// naive and passive died on w1) clear waves 1-6 at **33-50 s each**, while `notes/PROBE-PILOT.md` §13
// measures the ARRIVAL FLOOR — the wave with every body killed the frame it lands — at **60.6 s at w5
// and 66.8 s at w25**. A threshold in SECONDS is therefore reachable early and impossible deep, which is
// #206's criterion B failing by construction. The same page settles what to use instead: *"an
// undecomposed clear time is ~95% scheduler"*, so the kill-bound part is the ~5% the pilot owns.
//   EXCESS = bell − last arrival, IN SECONDS. **The first cut divided it by the wave's total and priced a
//   SHARE, and `[CLN]` killed that in one run: a wave whose bodies all arrive at once scored 0.999 and
//   could never earn the bonus however fast it was cleared.** The error is worth writing down because it
//   looked like the careful choice — the excess is ALREADY scheduler-free, since every second of schedule
//   falls before the last arrival, so dividing by the total puts the scheduler BACK IN and punishes a wave
//   for arriving quickly. Seconds of excess is the scale-free quantity; the share was scale-free-looking.
//   THE EDGES ARE THE FLOOR'S OWN SLACK: §13 measures an undecomposed clear time at ~95% scheduler, so the
//   kill-bound remainder of a ~60s wave is **~3 s**. FULL at 3 s is exactly that — a wave killed as fast as
//   it arrives — and HALF at 8 s is a pilot who is late but not idle.
// THE PAY is a third of `W1_BONUS`: first blood is once a RUN, this is once a WAVE and lands ~20 times,
// so a third of a one-off is already the larger lifetime line. ◆10 is also `wstylePop`'s watch, so the
// banner's number is a value a pilot has already learned to read. Pinned in three places.
const CLEAN = { FULL: 3.0, HALF: 8.0, PAY: 10 };   // SECONDS of kill-bound excess, not a share — see above
