//  THE REFLECT ORB (BOOK II #111) — the rabbi's cage, and it is MIRORB RE-PARAMETERISED, not a second system.
//  Every number below is stated against the boss's, because criterion B asks which of his changed and why:
//    CAP 4 -> 2   a boss may bank four; a WAVE BODY that banked four would BE a boss. Half, so the pilot can
//                 empty it inside one magazine and feel the bank fill while he does.
//    R 100 -> 76  `SHK.ABSORB_R` and `MIRORB.R` are BOTH 100, and criterion D says pick the orb's on purpose:
//                 smaller, because a wave body must be FLANKABLE. At 76 a pilot can shoot past the orb at the
//                 rabbi's own hitbox from the side; at 100 he could not, and the rung would be a wall.
//    DUR 3.2 -> 2.6, CD 8 -> 7   shorter window, shorter wait. The boss's orb is a PHASE; a rabbi's is a moment.
//    HOLD 0.4 -> dropped         that is the mirror's phase settle and the rabbi has no phase to settle into.
//
//  **THE TERMINATION LAW IS INHERITED, NOT RE-DERIVED** (criterion A): the guard is the same `if (b.reflected)`
//  clause the other two cages carry — a record with `reflected >= 1` is eaten DEAD by ANY cage, so a rabbi orb
//  reflecting into the pilot's cage terminates instead of playing tennis. It is a repeated clause per collision
//  loop rather than one function, which is why it must be written at each door and why a law must prove it once.
//
//  **AND NOTHING IS MINTED AT THE REFLECT DOOR** (criterion C): one record in, one record out. The orb splices
//  the bolt it ate and pushes exactly one, carrying `reflected: 1`; it never manufactures damage and never emits
//  more than it swallowed. A reflected pulse hurts the pilot through `hurtPlayer` — the one door for hearts —
//  exactly as the mirror's does.
//
//  WHICH WEAPONS IT ANSWERS (criterion D), and the counterplay IS the answer: **it REFLECTS the PULSE and
//  ABSORBS NOTHING ELSE.** Rockets, storm bolts and shadow pass straight through to the body. Shooting the orb
//  with your main gun FEEDS it; every other weapon in the kit ignores it. That is what keeps it from being a
//  free block — the pilot is never locked out, he is asked to change tools.
const RBORB = { CAP: 2, R: 76, DUR: 2.6, CD: 7, VENT: 1.3 };
// (#114) THE CAGE'S VOICE. **THERE WAS NO ORBIT HUM.** `#111` gave the cage a mechanic, `#112` gave it ink, `#113`
//  gave it a window — and for three stones it made ONE sound in its life: a 660→990Hz sine at the open, then 2.6
//  seconds of silence while it ate the pilot's shots. A pilot watching his own feet had no way to know.
//
//  **WHERE IT SITS, WHICH IS CRITERION B'S WHOLE QUESTION.** THE PRESENCE HUM owns the bottom of this family's mix:
//  a 90→84Hz SINE at vol 0.022, re-fired at most every 0.8s while any rabbi is firing (`game.js` ~1275 — NOT
//  `:1096`, which is jack-boy rush code). The cage sits **clear of it on all three axes at once**, because pitch
//  alone is a thin separation when both sounds are quiet drones:
//    · PITCH: 196→185Hz, a shade over 2.18x the hum's fundamental. Far enough that nothing beats against it —
//      two drones a few Hz apart would fuse into one wobbling tone and the pilot would learn neither.
//    · TIMBRE: TRIANGLE, not sine. The presence hum has no harmonics above its fundamental; this one does, so the
//      two are separable by COLOUR even when the mix is crowded and both are near the noise floor.
//    · CADENCE: 0.55s re-fired every 0.5s, which overlaps into a SUSTAIN. The presence hum pulses every 0.8s with
//      0.3s of gap. One breathes, one holds — and "held" is the truth: the cage is a state, not an event.
//  LEVEL, AND THE RENDER CORRECTED ME. I designed this as "between them" from the `vol` fields — above the presence
//  hum's 0.022, below the eat click's 0.04. **MEASURED, that ranking is wrong**: presence peaks -38.52 dBFS, the hum
//  -37.49, the click -42.65. The click is the QUIETEST of the three, not the loudest, because 0.06s of square never
//  reaches the amplitude its `vol` implies — and the hum's RMS (-53.03) sits 3.5 dB BELOW presence (-49.56) even
//  though its peak is 1 dB above, which is a triangle's crest factor and not something a `vol` field can tell you.
//  **SO THE HONEST CLAIM IS NOT A LEVEL RANKING AT ALL, IT IS SEPARATION** — the spectrograms show the presence hum
//  confined to 84-90Hz with NOTHING above 250Hz, and the cage's fundamental starting at 185Hz with harmonics at
//  590/980/1400Hz. The two occupy DISJOINT bands, which is a stronger answer to criterion B than any dB gap, and
//  the click lives alone at 620-1500Hz plus a stack to 20kHz where it cuts through by brightness rather than level.
//  The click stays quietest on purpose: the HUM carries the state and the VENT carries the break, so the click is
//  the least load-bearing of the family and its level is the one that may be low.
//
//  ONE HUM AT A TIME, GLOBALLY (`RBWAR.orbHumT`). A firing line can field several gilded rabbis and several cages;
//  N stacked drones is a wall, not a warning. The nearest caged body to reach the check wins the slot, so the hum
//  is a WARNING rather than a positional cue — said plainly because it is a real limitation of the choice.
const RBORB_SFX = { F0: 196, F1: 185, DUR: 0.55, VOL: 0.03, EVERY: 0.5 };
// (#113) THE COUNTERPLAY WINDOW. **`VENT` IS NOT A NEW NUMBER AND THAT IS THE WHOLE RULING.** The rabbi already
//  owns a punish window: `e.state = 'vent'` holds **1.3s** after every beam cutoff (*"overheated: the punish window
//  — he can do NOTHING here"*), it has a four-frame `rb_vent_0..3` animation on the sheet, its own `SFX.rbVent`,
//  and `rabbisim [47]` already proves it as **78 frames with full damage through**. The sweeper's own line says it
//  out loud: *"his 1.3s vent recovery IS your window."* So the cage's window is the SAME 1.3s, because a pilot
//  should learn ONE punish length for this family and not two.
//
//  **WHAT IT FIXES IS A LIE I SHIPPED.** `#112` made the bank countable and its gallery grade said *"a full cage is
//  about to stop eating, which is when he should shoot."* It was not true: the door reads
//  `if (e.orb.bank < RBORB.CAP) e.orb.bank++`, so the bank saturated at 2 and the cage kept eating forever. The
//  readout was a readout of nothing. **Feeding it to CAP now BURSTS it** — the sentence is true rather than deleted.
//
//  **VENT < CD ON PURPOSE, AND THAT IS THE JUDGEMENT (criterion C: difficulty is time and judgement).** Waiting out
//  a natural expiry buys the pilot the full `CD` 7s with no risk. Bursting buys 1.3s, arrives ~6x sooner, and costs
//  him TWO returned bolts to dodge. Neither dominates: the two paths land within a few percent of the same
//  shooting uptime and differ entirely in risk and tempo. If `VENT` were `CD`, bursting would strictly dominate
//  waiting and the cage would stop being a decision.
//
//  NOT PRICED, DELIBERATELY: no damage, no speed, no hp. The orb gains nothing — `[M2]`'s doctrine holds the
//  enemy's numbers to the pilot's, and a window is time, which is the currency that doctrine actually permits.
// (#112) THE ORB'S INK. **`v1.6.716` shipped this cage with ZERO draw calls** — a pilot was feeding something he
//  could not see. The renderer is the RETIRED `drawGuardRing`'s idiom, not a new one (that ring came off
//  his body this stone; the pattern outlived it here): polygon annulus built with
//  moveTo/lineTo and closed with `fill()`, a DARK SHELL plate first and oversized for keyline contrast (the
//  answer to `PATTERNS.md` (3)), and `HUDLAB.probe` bookkeeping so a law can read what was painted.
//
//  **`fill()` IS THE VISIBLE CASE AND THE ORDER SAYS THE OPPOSITE.** D128: the draw recorder books `fill()` over a
//  built path and books NEITHER `stroke()` NOR gradients NOR composite ops. The shipped mirror orb ring is
//  STROKED, which is why no law has ever been able to hold it. Nothing load-bearing here is stroked.
//
//  **AND THERE IS NO GAP, DELIBERATELY.** The hit test is a FULL CIRCLE, so a drawn opening would tell a pilot he
//  can shoot through a place where he cannot — the same lie `RBORB.R = 76` was chosen to avoid. The queue's very
//  next stone is THE COUNTERPLAY WINDOWS, whose job is an honest opening; this stone makes legible only what
//  exists: WHERE the cage is, WHETHER it is up, and HOW FULL its bank is.
//
//  R IS `RBORB.R` ITSELF, READ AND NEVER RETYPED: the drawn outer edge is the radius that actually eats bolts.
const RBORB_ART = {
  T: 7,        // annulus thickness, px — thick enough to survive the keyline and stay a ring, not a hairline
  KEY: 2,      // the dark shell's overhang either side, px — `GUARD.KEY` is 1.6 at a 44px hoop; this hoop is 76
  SPIN: 0.9,   // rad/s. `GUARD.SPIN` is 0.5 and its comment is the reason this exists at all: "a ring that does
  //             not turn reads as a decal stuck to the camera, not an object." Faster than the pilot's because a
  //             cage that is EATING should look busier than one that is merely worn.
  STEPS: 48,   // polygon segments per FULL TURN, spent PRO-RATA by arc — never a fixed count per arc.
  //             **THE FIRST RENDER FAILED THIS RING'S OWN COMMENT.** It read `STEPS: 12` and `plate` spent all
  //             twelve on whatever arc it was given, so the two full-circle plates came out as visible DODECAGONS
  //             with flat facets and corners at 76px, while a 0.11-rad notch got the same twelve it did not need.
  //             the retired guard ring got away with a fixed count because every one of its arcs was a SHORT keyed
  //             segment of a 44px hoop; this hoop is a continuous 76px circle and the same number is a polygon.
  //             Caught by looking at the photograph, which is the only instrument that can see a facet.
  NOTCH: 6,    // rotating tick marks on the annulus: the motion cue that does not imply an opening
  NW: 0.055,   // each notch's angular half-width, rad
  BURST: 0.3,  // the burst flash's life, s — `GUARD.CRACK` is 0.3 and this is the same event: a shield breaking.
  //             Reused rather than re-picked, so the two breaks in this game read at the same speed.
  BLOW: 3,     // how far the cage flies apart, in multiples of `T` — far enough to read as debris, near enough
  //             that a pilot can still name where the cage WAS. Derived from the art table, not from taste.
};
function rbOrbLive(e) { return !!(e && e.orb && e.orb.t > gameTime); }
