//               one wave and a curve there could never move.
//   `open`    — the row's own side effect (the tear, the mirror). A composition table cannot express
//               "and also push a boss", so the hook is named data rather than a branch.
// EVERY row then passes through `wrunFit` — proportional trim, debut door, sky floor, conservation —
// which is the half that used to reach one arm in eight.
// ══ THE SPAWN RULES (BOOK II #179) — **THE FIGHT WAS UNREACHABLE AND THAT IS A GREP, NOT AN OPINION.**
//   `FORGE.show = 1` had **ZERO** occurrences in this file; the only writers were the `fgShow` dev dial and
//   the `= 0` at fight-end. Both occurrences of `forgeClockSet('run')` were **inside comments.** Eight
//   stones built this boss (#151-170) and eight more graded it (#171-178), and no run could ever produce
//   it — every frame in `gallery/stone-17*` was reached by a dial a pilot cannot press.
//   **THE DIAL'S OWN CAPTION SAID SO AND WENT STALE:** *"it SHIPS OFF and that is deliberate: the body is
//   cut, the event that spawns it is not built (stones 151+)."* The event is this stone.
//
// **THE SEAT IS 65, AND THE FIRST ANSWER WAS 45 UNTIL THE HARNESS REFUSED IT.** Named waves run
//   5 · 10 · 15 · 20 · 25 · 30(every 30) · 35 · 40, so 45 is the first free multiple of 5 above THE SUMMIT
//   and it was the obvious pick. **`wavesim [STMP]` crashed on it**: that law samples waves [32, 38, 45] as
//   LADDER waves for V10's borrowed ceilings, and a milestone row carries no `caps`. It was right and the
//   seat was wrong — naming a wave takes it out of the ladder, and w45 was somebody's ruler.
//   **SO THE SEAT WAS RE-DERIVED BY MEASUREMENT:** counting every wave number in a harness array literal
//   between w33 and w120, **w45 is sampled 34 times — the most-sampled late wave in the roster.** w65 is
//   sampled ZERO times and is the next multiple of 5 that is.
//   **AND `WSCHED`'s OWN COMMENT IS NOW FALSIFIED, which is worth more than this row:** it claims *"every
//   canary in the roster is at or below w32"*. `[STMP]` samples w45 and `[26]` samples past it. That
//   sentence was true when written and is not now; a stone naming a late wave must COUNT rather than trust it.
// **THE CADENCE NEVER MEETS THE MIRROR.** At 65-every-65 against LAB TRAX's 30-every-30, the first
//   shared wave is LCM(65,30) = **w390** — outside any run this game has seen. The row still sits AFTER the
//   mirror's in the array so that if a dial ever makes them collide, `wschedMile`'s first-match IS the
//   tie-break: the mirror opens and the forge waits. One boss per wave, by position, with no new branch.
const FORGE_SPAWN = { AT: 65, EVERY: 65 };
// **PER VARIANT WITHOUT TOUCHING A SINGLE VARIANT ROW.** Criterion C asks for a per-variant frequency, and
//   the obvious move — a new `forgeEvery` column across `WVARIANTS` — is the expensive one: `[IH1]` compares
//   the IRON HEART row against V1 **cell by cell**, so a column added to one row and not the other reds a
//   law that is right to red. Reading an OPTIONAL cell with a fallback costs no row an edit, lets any
//   future stone override exactly one variant, and every shipped variant reaches the forge today at the
//   dial's own value — which is the census criterion C asks for, and `[FSP]` clause 3 takes it.
function forgeEveryFor() {
  const v = wvar();
  const e = v && v.forgeEvery;
  return Number.isFinite(e) && e > 0 ? e : FORGE_SPAWN.EVERY;
}
// **AND THE SAME DOOR FOR `at`, BECAUSE `every` ALONE CANNOT OPEN IT (BOOK II #294).** The row matcher is
// `if (R.every) { if (n >= R.at && n % R.every === 0) return R; }` — so a variant that sets `forgeEvery: 1`
// and nothing else STILL waits for wave 65, because `at` is the gate and `every` only picks the cadence
// after it. THE BOSS DOOR needs wave ONE. This is the twin of the function above, written in its own idiom
// for its own stated reason: an OPTIONAL cell read with a fallback costs no existing row an edit, so
// `[IH1]`'s cell-by-cell V7-against-V1 comparison never sees it.
function forgeAtFor() {
  const v = wvar();
  const a = v && v.forgeAt;
  return Number.isFinite(a) && a > 0 ? a : FORGE_SPAWN.AT;
}
