// O(1) tests, where the old block ran both scans on every frame the queue was empty.
//
// WHAT IT CANNOT SEE (THE HUMILITY CLAUSE): whether the wave SHOULD be over. It reports the state of seven   // (BELLWATCH upkeep: the list had grown to seven and this word still said five)
// fields; it does not know if a body is unreachable, if a tear is sealing, or if a machine will ever land. It
// also cannot tell a reader HOW to clear what it names — which is why a fixture that force-clears the field
// can be told what it missed but can never be immune to a condition nothing retires on its own.
// THE COUNT BAR'S NUMBER (THE-TWO-FIFTY #164, stone 194) — what the bell is still waiting for.
//
// **"LIVE FROM CONSERVATION" IS NOT A STYLE OF COUNTING, IT IS A SHARED SOURCE.** This reads the SAME five
// collections `wrunBellHolds()` reads, in the same order, so the two can never disagree: the count is zero
// exactly when the bell releases. A second tally maintained beside the bell — incremented on spawn,
// decremented on death — is the obvious build and it is the one that rots. It would drift the first time a
// body left the field by a path nobody added a hook to, and it would drift SILENTLY, because a HUD number
// has no law of its own until someone writes one. **A derived count cannot drift from the thing it derives
// from.** `wavesim [CB]` murders the alternative by proving the equivalence directly.
//
// WHAT IT CANNOT SEE (THE HUMILITY CLAUSE): it counts what the BELL counts, which is not always what a
