// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// THE DECOY — A HOLOGRAM PILOT, AND AN ADDRESS THE STREET BELIEVES (THE-TWO-FIFTY #111, LEG 5's last power-up)
//
// ── CRITERION A, THE PRIOR-ART RULING, AND IT IS **EXTEND FOR ONE HALF AND NEW FOR THE OTHER.** The order
// names `flares` (`game.js` ~7856) — *"burning decoys adrift in the air — every homing thing loves them"* —
// and asks whether this is the flare's aggro with a different body. **It is not, and the search says exactly
// why: the flare's aggro reaches TWO consumers in the whole file.** A nearest-lie search inside the grudge's
// lock, and a second inside the homing-projectile loop (*"a flare in the air outshines the man"*). That is a
// COUNTERMEASURE — three in a rack, an 8-second recharge, `life 4.5`, adrift in the AIR — and it is believed
// only by things that home. **Nothing that walks has ever believed a flare.** The order's words are *"enemies
// address it under the targeting weights"*, which the flare path has never once done.
//   So: **EXTEND at both flare consumers** — the decoy joins the same nearest-lie search, one condition each,
// and a homing rocket now prefers whichever lie is closer. **NEW for the body half**, because a jack boy
// cannot be made to believe a flare without inventing the thing this block is: an ADDRESS.
//
// ── AND THE ADDRESS IS ONE TOKEN, WHICH IS #109's AND #110's LESSON APPLIED A THIRD TIME. `update()` already
// caches the pilot's centre once per frame as `pcx0`/`pcy0` and reads it **46 times**. Beside it now sits
// `adx0`/`ady0` — THE ADDRESS: the decoy when one is posted, the pilot otherwise. **Grep `adx0` and you have
// the decoy's entire reach.** A list in a comment goes stale; a token cannot.
//
// ── **THE THREE KINDS OF `pcx0` READ, AND ONLY ONE OF THEM MAY FOLLOW A LIE.** This is the load-bearing
// distinction of the stone and a blanket rename would have been a bug factory:
//   · **AIM and APPROACH** — where a body walks and where it throws. These follow the address. *(the decoy)*
//   · **SPACING, TOUCH and YIELD** — the bubbles that keep bodies off each other and off the pilot's plow.
//     These must NOT follow: a crowd that physically avoided a hologram and walked through the man would be
//     a worse game than no decoy at all.
//   · **SEEN gates and the loot magnet** — *"is the pilot near enough to act"*, and treasure flying home.
//     Neither is an address. A decoy that woke sleeping bodies would be a spawner, not a lie.
//
// ── **THE CONTACT TEST STAYS ON THE MAN, AND THAT IS THE ONE BUG THIS DESIGN WANTED TO SHIP.** `jackTick`
// takes its address as PARAMETERS (`pcx`, `pcy`), so handing it `adx0`/`ady0` turns the whole street brain —
// approach, commit, flank, berths, the blade's own swing — onto the decoy for free. But its `contact()` helper
// tests `Math.abs(pcx - e.x) < reach` and then calls `hurtPlayer()`, so with the address swapped a boy
// standing next to the HOLOGRAM would have hurt the pilot across the street. `contact()` now reads
// `player.x`/`player.y` directly. **A body swings at the lie and hits nothing, which is the mechanic.**
//
// ── THE LIFE IS THE SLOT, AND NO CLOCK WAS BUILT (#109's rule). `decoyLive()` reads `pactLeft('decoy') > 0`,
// so the store's 8 seconds ARE the body's 8 seconds, the HUD chip is correct by construction, and the pop
// fires when the slot empties. `DECOY.t` is a SHIMMER PHASE and not a life — it is declared WORLD, advanced by
// the world's dt, because a hologram standing in the street is a thing in the world and not a referee.
//
