// exactly ONE predicate and both callers read it. THE BOUNCE runs on a LIVE wave run and on the dev
// desk, and nowhere else: not the story (which has POWER SURGE and is not this leg's subject), not
// the setter's back room, not the variant gate, and not under a wave-mode screen — `WMODE.screen`
// covers the shop, the death slab and the confirm-quit, and a prize rolling behind a menu is a prize
// the pilot loses to paperwork.
//
// WHAT THE STAGE REPORT FOUND NEXT DOOR, WHICH IS WHY THAT PARAGRAPH IS THIS LONG (criterion A).
// `updatePowerGem(dt)` was called unconditionally from `update()` (game.js:17977 then; since v1.6.1440 `update()` skips it on the
// wave stage — his order 2026-09-06, THE POWER GEM IS THE STORY'S — so what follows describes the story's doors) and its draw is
// fenced on `bossReady` — an IMAGE-LOAD flag, not a stage — so POWER SURGE is live, drawn and
// collectible on ALL FOUR doors: story, wave, setter and sandbox. Measured, not read: the gem armed
// within ~4 s of every door and its halo arc came back in the recorder on each. `placePowerGem()`
// picks from `platforms.filter(kind === 'bsurf')`, and `buildStreet()` runs at MODULE TOP LEVEL
// (game.js:13425), so all 131 city rooftops exist on the sand too and the gem floats at a rooftop
// height over open desert with nothing under it. `lootDrops` already knows better — game.js:19368
// reads `if (!supported && !desertStage())`, commented "the desert has no ledges (#5): a diamond
// never floats on an invisible rooftop" — and the gem has no such clause. Left ALONE per criterion B;
// reported in RUN-LOG, dispatched, and on the RETURN LEDGER with its one-line cure.
//
// THE PHYSICS, and every number below is a dial with a caption on THE POWER BENCH.
//   fall       `SKY` px above the street, `vy = 0`, `vx = ±DRIFT`
//   gravity    `G` = 1300 px/s² — REUSED from lootDrops' own 1300 (game.js:19361) on purpose, so the
//              two falling families read as the same world and not as two authors
//   floor      `vy = -vy * DECAY` and `hops++`; apex heights fall by DECAY² = 0.36 each hop, so at
//              SKY 720 the arc is 259 → 93 → 34 → 12 px over four hops. Geometric, so bounded.
//   walls      at `x0 ± BOX`, `vx = -vx * WALL`, unbounded in count while `hops < HOPS`
//   the roll   on the HOPS-th floor contact the vertical is retired and `vx` is set to ±ROLL — the
//              walls go OPEN and the prize leaves under its own steam. This is the whole reason the
//              order's "wall rebounds" and "despawns off-edge" are not in contradiction: the walls
//              hold a LIVE prize in the pilot's reach, and let a SPENT one go.
//   exits      COLLECTED (`GRAB` px, centre to centre) or MISSED (past `BOX + EDGE`, or `LIFE` out).
//              `LIFE` is a backstop and not the design: at shipped numbers a missed prize exits
//              off-edge at ~6.4 s against a 9 s clock, so the clock should never be the door. If it
//              ever is, `PRIZE.led.expired` counts it and the law reds — a silent leak is the one
//              failure that would break #114's conservation (spawned = collected + missed).
//
// WHAT THIS ENGINE CANNOT SEE (THE HUMILITY CLAUSE): it knows nothing about what a prize DOES. The
// effect is `KINDS[k].take()`, a hook, and #99 ships `gold` with a body, a sting and an empty hook —
// #104 fills it with the shield. So a green `[PB*]` proves prizes spawn, bounce, rebound, roll, leave
// and collect; it proves NOTHING about a power-up's effect, and it never will.
const PRIZE = {
  G: 1300,        // gravity, px/s² — lootDrops' own constant, deliberately shared
  SKY: 720,       // drop height above the street, px
  DECAY: 0.60,    // vertical restitution per floor contact
  WALL: 0.72,     // horizontal restitution per wall contact
  HOPS: 4,        // floor contacts before the vertical is retired and the roll begins
  BOX: 420,       // half-width of the play box, measured from the spawn x
  EDGE: 120,      // margin past the box that counts as off-edge
  DRIFT: 170,     // horizontal speed out of the sky, px/s
  ROLL: 210,      // the roll's speed once the hops are spent, px/s
  GRAB: 46,       // collect radius, px, centre to centre
  LIFE: 9,        // the backstop clock, s — never the intended door
  SPIN: 2.4,      // tumble, rad/s
  ROCK: 0.42,     // rad — the HALF-ANGLE a live prize may turn through (BOOK II #443, MOVED HERE AT #454).
                  // Past about 0.6 the girdle foreshortens enough that a wide cut reads as a narrow one and
                  // the kind's second signal is gone; at 0 the stone is a sticker. 0.42 is 24 degrees each way.
                  // ⚠⚠ IT LIVED IN `MIRSHARD` FOR ELEVEN SHIPS AND NOBODY NOTICED, INCLUDING ME WHO PUT IT
                  //   THERE. #443 anchored an edit on `SPIN: 3.4,      // top tumble rate, rad/s` — and
                  //   `MIRSHARD` carries a line matching that text exactly, one const above this one. So
                  //   `PRIZE.ROCK` read `undefined`, `Math.sin(x) * undefined` is NaN, and **the canvas spec
                  //   says `rotate(NaN)` is SILENTLY DISCARDED** — no throw, no warning, no rotation.
                  //   **AIRBORNE PRIZES HAVE NEVER ROTATED SINCE #443**, and that stone's own published
                  //   receipt — *"the natural-angle frame now looks like the matched-rotation frame"* — was
                  //   TRUE FOR THE WRONG REASON: the two frames agreed because rotation was off entirely.
                  //   A silent no-op is the worst failure shape there is: it looks exactly like success.
  // #114's conservation ledger. THE INVARIANT IS `spawned === collected + missed`, exactly as the order
  // states it, and it holds through every exit including a door change — `cleared` is a SUB-COUNT of
  // `missed`, not a third bucket, so the identity never needs a footnote. Found by measurement, not by
  // reading: the first cut incremented `spawned` and then dropped the field on a stage change without
  // counting the drop, and the story arm of the arc probe came back `spawned:1, collected:0, missed:0`.
  // #114 would have inherited a conservation law that was false at the one seam nobody stages.
  led: { spawned: 0, collected: 0, missed: 0, expired: 0, cleared: 0, hops: 0, walls: 0, caught: 0 },   // `caught` (#104) is a SUB-COUNT of `collected`, never a third bucket — the invariant is untouched
};
// THE GLINT'S NUMBERS (#103). Not on THE POWER BENCH and that is #112's row to add — every dial in this
// file reads through a `SANDBOX.on` gate, so a desk dial for a body drawn in a wave run would be inert
// where it matters (the same argument #100's rarity table made, and the same murder ROOM 20 lost).
const GLINT = {
  SPEED: 1.15,   // sweeps per second of a prize's own age
  WIDE: 0.17,    // the haze's half-width, as a fraction of the face
  CORE: 0.055,   // ...and the hot core's
  HAZE: 0.30,    // the haze's alpha
  HOT: 0.82,     // the core's
  LEAN: 0.16,    // how far the bottom edge LAGS the top, in sweep units — light crossing a bevel leans
};
const prizes = [];   // {kind, x, y, x0, vx, vy, hops, spin, age, rolling, dead}
// THE KINDS REGISTRY. #103–#111 each add one row. `body(g, s)` draws at the origin already translated
// and rotated; `take()` is the effect hook and may be empty while its own stone is unbuilt.
const PRIZE_KINDS = {
  gold: {
    key: 'gold', label: 'GOLD BAR', tint: '#ffd24a',
    // THE TRAVELING FACE GLINT (#103, and the third argument is why this signature grew). `ph` is a
    // 0..1 PHASE, not a clock: `drawPrizes` derives it from the prize's OWN age, so two bars in the air
    // never glint in lockstep — a sheen synchronised across every ingot on screen reads as a screen
    // effect rather than as light on metal.
    //   · IT IS A QUAD ACROSS THE TRAPEZOID, NOT A CLIPPED RECT, and that is a constraint rather than a
    //     preference: the face is a trapezoid, and `ctx.clip()` does not exist in the harness context at
    //     all — a clipped band would paint correctly in a browser and outside the bar in every law. So
    //     the band interpolates the face's own top and bottom edges and is inside BY CONSTRUCTION.
    //   · THE BOTTOM LAGS THE TOP by `GL.LEAN` of a sweep, so the band leans the way a real highlight
    //     leans when light crosses a bevel — a vertical bar would read as a wipe.
    //   · TWO PASSES: a wide soft haze and a narrow hot core. One pass reads as a paint stripe.
    //   · IT SWEEPS OUT PAST BOTH EDGES (the phase runs -0.25..1.25) so the glint ENTERS and LEAVES
    //     rather than being born and dying inside the face.
    body: (g, s, ph) => {   // a struck ingot: lit top face, warm front, dark under-shadow, a hard highlight, and a glint that travels
      const w = 30 * s, h = 15 * s, tp = 7 * s;
      g.fillStyle = '#8a5c06';                                                   // the under-shadow
      g.beginPath(); g.moveTo(-w, h); g.lineTo(w, h); g.lineTo(w - tp, h + 4 * s); g.lineTo(-w + tp, h + 4 * s); g.closePath(); g.fill();
      g.fillStyle = '#e0a316';                                                   // the front face
      g.beginPath(); g.moveTo(-w, -h); g.lineTo(w, -h); g.lineTo(w - tp, h); g.lineTo(-w + tp, h); g.closePath(); g.fill();
      // THE KEYLINE, AND IT IS THE FRAMES' OWN FINDING RATHER THAN A FLOURISH. BAR 3 of this stone's
      // direction grades the order's superlative — "the most collectible-looking object in the game" — by
      // photographing the bar BESIDE a diamond, and the first take said something worth keeping: the bar
      // wins on desirability (it is four times the size and it is gold) and LOSES ON ATTENTION, because a
      // 14px ice-white gem against warm sand out-contrasts a 60px warm ingot against warm sand. That is
      // `PATTERNS.md` (3) for the fourth time in this tree — a warm mark measured against a backdrop it
      // does not lie on — and the cheapest cure in game art is the oldest one: a dark keyline that gives
      // the silhouette its own edge whatever it stands on. It costs one stroked path, it changes none of
      // #99's three tones (which passed their own grade), and it is drawn UNDER the bevel and the glint so
      // it edges the shape without touching the light on it.
      g.strokeStyle = 'rgba(46,28,2,0.85)'; g.lineWidth = Math.max(1, 1.6 * s);
      g.beginPath(); g.moveTo(-w, -h); g.lineTo(w, -h); g.lineTo(w - tp, h); g.lineTo(-w + tp, h); g.closePath(); g.stroke();
      g.fillStyle = '#ffd24a';                                                   // the lit top
      g.beginPath(); g.moveTo(-w + tp, -h); g.lineTo(w - tp, -h); g.lineTo(w - tp * 1.7, -h - 6 * s); g.lineTo(-w + tp * 1.7, -h - 6 * s); g.closePath(); g.fill();
      g.fillStyle = 'rgba(255,244,194,0.85)';                                    // the highlight
      g.fillRect(-w + tp * 1.3, -h + 2 * s, (w - tp * 1.3) * 1.1, 2.2 * s);
      if (ph !== undefined) {   // ...and the glint, which travels
        const u = -0.25 + (ph % 1) * 1.5;                                        // enters at -0.25 and leaves at 1.25 — a sweep with an entrance and an exit
        const band = (uu, half, col) => {
          const a = uu - half, b = uu + half;
          if (b <= 0 || a >= 1) return;                                          // wholly off the face: nothing to paint, and no zero-area quad in the books
          const ca = Math.max(0, Math.min(1, a)), cb = Math.max(0, Math.min(1, b));
          const xt = (q) => -w + q * 2 * w;                                      // the face's TOP edge, -w..w
          const xb = (q) => -w + tp + q * 2 * (w - tp);                          // ...and its BOTTOM edge, inset by the bevel
          const lean = GLINT.LEAN;
          g.fillStyle = col;
          g.beginPath();
          g.moveTo(xt(ca), -h); g.lineTo(xt(cb), -h);
          g.lineTo(xb(Math.max(0, Math.min(1, cb - lean))), h);
          g.lineTo(xb(Math.max(0, Math.min(1, ca - lean))), h);
          g.closePath(); g.fill();
        };
        band(u, GLINT.WIDE, 'rgba(255,246,205,' + GLINT.HAZE + ')');             // the haze
        band(u, GLINT.CORE, 'rgba(255,255,240,' + GLINT.HOT + ')');              // the hot core
      }
    },
    // #104 THE GOLD BAR ② THE SHIELD fills this. #99 shipped the engine, not the effect — and the effect
    // is TWO LINES, because criterion B's prior art did the work: the guard, its absorb, its HUD hearts
    // and its death-clear all pre-date this stone, so the bar's whole job is to feed the one grant door.
    // NO COLLECT VOICE IS ADDED HERE, deliberately: `prizeTake` already rings two tones for the pickup,
    // #113 THE POWER SOUNDS owns the per-type collect stings, and the thing that tells the pilot he has a
    // shield is the RING APPEARING around him — which is the order's own word, "visible".
    take: (p) => {
      shieldGuard(GUARD.HITS);
      // THE VOICES RETURN (his word, 2026-08-30, v1.6.1354): "for power ups, if i pick them up i do
      //   want an announcement for what i got" — every take speaks its name on the corner announce
      //   lane, gated REWARD. A refused gate is never silence: prizeTake's #444 identity line below
      //   still prints the label on the ticker when the take pushed nothing it recognises.
      if (annAllow('REWARD', 'prz-gold')) floaters.push({ lane: 'announce', pr: 2, x: p.x, y: p.y - 130, text: 'GOLD GUARD +' + GUARD.HITS, age: 0, big: true });
      SFX.przGold(p.x);   // (#113) THE ARRIVAL. Until this stone the wave-1 GUARANTEED prize made no sound at all — `shieldGuard` is silent — so the first prize every run meets had no voice.
    },
  },
  // ── RAPID FIRE (#105). THE SHAPE IS THE ORDER'S — "a crossed-bolts body" — AND IT IS DRAWN AS TWO
  // ZIG-ZAG BOLTS AND NOT AS AN X, which is the distinction BAR 1 exists to fail. Each bolt has a KINK
  // (a lightning bolt's whole silhouette is its kink) and the two cross near their middles.
  //   · **AMBER, NOT GOLD, AND THAT IS BAR 2's REQUIREMENT RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE.** From this ship there
  //     are TWO prize kinds in the air, and if a pilot cannot tell which is falling, the rarity table is
  //     decoration. Hue alone would not do it — a busy frame takes hue first — so the SILHOUETTES differ:
  //     the bar is a solid horizontal trapezoid, this is an open diagonal cross of thin strokes.
  //   · **THE DARK KEYLINE IS #104's LESSON, TWO HOURS OLD.** A warm mark on warm sand loses its edge
  //     (`PATTERNS.md` (3), five cases in this tree), so every stroke is laid over a darker one first.
  //   · IT DOES NOT USE `stroke()`: filled quads, so `harness/env.js`'s recorder can see it (D128's limit).
  rapid: {
    key: 'rapid', label: 'RAPID FIRE', tint: '#ffb648',
    // THE FIRST CUT WAS A STROKED ZIG-ZAG AND THE PHOTOGRAPH KILLED IT. Three quads laid along a kinked
    // path at 3px wide over a 26px span read as **a fat X with a wobble** — BAR 1's own named failure, word
    // for word ("a plain X ... or the kinks are lost"). The stroke was wider than the kink it was supposed
    // to show. So the bolt is a SOLID SILHOUETTE now, the classic seven-point lightning polygon, where the
    // zig-zag IS the outline instead of being a wiggle inside a thick line.
    //   · **THE POINTS ARE PRE-ROTATED IN JS, AND THAT IS AN INSTRUMENT REQUIREMENT RATHER THAN A STYLE.**
    //     `harness/env.js` tracks `translate`, `scale` and `setTransform` in its matrix and does NOT handle
    //     `rotate` — so a `g.rotate()` here would rotate in a browser and NOT in the recorder, and every law
    //     would measure a shape no pilot ever sees. That is `ctx.clip()`'s trap (#103) in a second dress.
    //     Rotating the points myself makes the law's witness identical to the browser's geometry.
    //   · TWO BOLTS AT ±38°, crossing near their middles, the far one a tone down so the cross reads as
    //     DEPTH rather than as a flat emblem, and the near one carrying the hot edge.
    body: (g, s) => {
      const BOLT = [[-4, -15], [6, -15], [0.5, -3], [7, -3], [-4, 15], [0, 1], [-7, 1]];
      const put = (rot, col, sc, ox) => {
        const c = Math.cos(rot), sn = Math.sin(rot);
        g.fillStyle = col;
        g.beginPath();
        for (let i = 0; i < BOLT.length; i++) {
          const x = BOLT[i][0] * sc, y = BOLT[i][1] * sc;
          const rx = ((x * c - y * sn) + (ox || 0)) * s, ry = (x * sn + y * c) * s;
          if (i === 0) g.moveTo(rx, ry); else g.lineTo(rx, ry);
        }
        g.closePath(); g.fill();
      };
      // AND THE ANGLE IS THE SECOND THING THE PHOTOGRAPH MOVED. At ±38 degrees the two bolts overlapped
      // along most of their length and the frame read as ONE bolt with a shadow — better than the fat X it
      // replaced, and still not the order's word, which is "crossed". At ±53 they are 106 degrees apart:
      // they meet at the middle and part everywhere else, so both silhouettes are whole.
      // AND THE THIRD MOVE, WHICH IS THE ONE THAT MADE IT READ: the two bolts are OFFSET a few px apart
      // instead of concentric, so they cross at a visible POINT and each silhouette runs clear of the other
      // at both ends. Concentric, the near bolt's keyline swallowed the far bolt's body along the whole
      // overlap and the frame showed one bolt with a shadow. The far tone also came UP — `#a85f12` read as
      // shadow rather than as amber, which is the same "warm but too dark to be the colour" mistake
      // `PATTERNS.md` (3) keeps producing, aimed this time at a mark's own second half.
      put(-0.92, 'rgba(46,28,2,0.9)', 1.34, 3.2); put(0.92, 'rgba(46,28,2,0.9)', 1.34, -3.2);   // keylines, far first
      put(-0.92, '#e0902a', 1.34, 3.2);                                                 // the FAR bolt, one tone down — amber, not brown
      put(0.92, '#ffb648', 1.34, -3.2);                                                 // the NEAR bolt, full amber, over it
      put(0.92, 'rgba(255,246,210,0.85)', 0.70, -3.2);                                  // and its hot core, inset — a bolt is lit from inside
    },
    take: (p) => {
      pactArm('rapid');
      if (annAllow('REWARD', 'prz-rapid')) floaters.push({ lane: 'announce', pr: 2, x: p.x, y: p.y - 130, text: 'RAPID FIRE', age: 0, big: true, color: '#ffb648' });
      SFX.przRapid(p.x);
    },
  },
  // ── FREEZE FIELD (#106). A SIX-POINT SNOWFLAKE, and the shape is a ruling: the order says "ice-blue
  // body" and names no form, so BAR 2 chose it — **RADIAL**, where the gold bar is a solid horizontal
  // trapezoid and RAPID FIRE is an angular diagonal cross. Three prize kinds are in the air from this ship
  // and a busy frame takes HUE first, so the silhouettes have to separate before the colours do. It also
  // has to stay clear of the POWER GEM, a cut lozenge in the same `#6ef0ff` family — radial vs lozenge.
  //   · Each arm is a filled quad with two barbs, drawn from PRE-ROTATED points: `harness/env.js` tracks
  //     `translate`/`scale`/`setTransform` and NOT `rotate`, so a `g.rotate()` here would turn the shape in
  //     a browser and not in the recorder, and every law would measure geometry no pilot sees (#105's
  //     finding, `ctx.clip()`'s trap in a third dress).
  //   · The dark keyline is #104's lesson: ice-blue on pale desert SKY is the same warm-on-warm problem
  //     wearing the opposite temperature, and a falling prize crosses sky before it ever reaches sand.
  freeze: {
    key: 'freeze', label: 'FREEZE FIELD', tint: '#8fe4ff',
    body: (g, s) => {
      const arm = (rot, col, w, len, k) => {
        const c = Math.cos(rot), sn = Math.sin(rot);
        const put = (x, y) => { const rx = (x * c - y * sn) * s, ry = (x * sn + y * c) * s; g.lineTo(rx, ry); };
        g.fillStyle = col;
        g.beginPath();
        g.moveTo((-w * c) * s, (-w * sn) * s);
        put(w, -w * 0.0); put(len, -w); put(len, w); put(w, w * 0.0);
        g.closePath(); g.fill();
        // the two barbs — a snowflake without them is a star, which is BAR 1's own named failure
        for (const at of [0.46, 0.74]) {
          for (const side of [-1, 1]) {
            g.beginPath();
            const bx = len * at, by = 0;
            const tx = bx + len * 0.17, ty = side * len * 0.17;
            const rp = (x, y) => { const rx = (x * c - y * sn) * s, ry = (x * sn + y * c) * s; return [rx, ry]; };
            const a1 = rp(bx, by - k * 0.5), a2 = rp(tx, ty), a3 = rp(bx + k, by + k * 0.5);
            g.moveTo(a1[0], a1[1]); g.lineTo(a2[0], a2[1]); g.lineTo(a3[0], a3[1]);
            g.closePath(); g.fill();
          }
        }
      };
      // THE ARMS ARE THIN, AND THE FIRST CUT IS WHY. At w 2.4 over a 16px arm the six wedges merged with
      // their own barbs and the frame read as a cyan BURST — BAR 1's named failure, "a star ... rather than
      // a snowflake". A flake is delicate: the six-fold symmetry only reads when there is SAND BETWEEN THE
      // ARMS. Halving the widths and shortening the barbs is the whole cure.
      for (let i = 0; i < 6; i++) arm(i * Math.PI / 3, 'rgba(6,26,40,0.9)', 2.3, 19.5, 2.0);   // the keyline pass, wider and longer, underneath
      for (let i = 0; i < 6; i++) arm(i * Math.PI / 3, '#8fe4ff', 1.3, 18, 1.2);               // the ice
      for (let i = 0; i < 6; i++) arm(i * Math.PI / 3, 'rgba(240,253,255,0.92)', 0.6, 8, 0.6); // the lit core, short — a flake is brightest at its middle
    },
    take: (p) => {
      pactArm('freeze');
      if (annAllow('REWARD', 'prz-freeze')) floaters.push({ lane: 'announce', pr: 2, x: p.x, y: p.y - 130, text: 'FREEZE FIELD', age: 0, big: true, color: '#8fe4ff' });
      SFX.przFreeze(p.x);
      duckMusic('freeze');   // "the world goes QUIET-crackly" — the quiet half is the shipped sidechain, the crackle half is the seize's own visual on every body at once
    },
  },
  // ── GOLDEN TOUCH (#107). A COIN STACK, the order's own word, and the fourth silhouette in a set that now
  // has to stay legible four ways: the gold bar is a WIDE flat trapezoid, rapid an angular diagonal cross,
  // freeze a radial flake — so this is a VERTICAL column of discs. Tall where the bar is wide, which is the
  // separation that survives when hue does not.
  //   · The coins are ELLIPSES-as-quads (a squashed hexagon per disc): the recorder logs `fill()` over a
  //     built path and does NOT log `ellipse`, so a real ellipse here would be invisible to every law in the
  //     building (D128's stated limit, third stone running).
  //   · Warm gold, but a DIFFERENT warm from the ingot's: the bar is `#ffd24a` flat plate, this is stacked
  //     discs with a dark gap between each, so the read is texture rather than tone.
  gtouch: {
    key: 'gtouch', label: 'GOLDEN TOUCH', tint: '#ffcf3d',
    body: (g, s) => {
      const disc = (cy, rx, ry, col) => {
        g.fillStyle = col;
        g.beginPath();
        for (let i = 0; i <= 12; i++) {
          const a = (i / 12) * Math.PI * 2;
          const x = Math.cos(a) * rx * s, y = cy * s + Math.sin(a) * ry * s;
          if (i === 0) g.moveTo(x, y); else g.lineTo(x, y);
        }
        g.closePath(); g.fill();
      };
      const N = 5, step = 5.4;
      for (let i = N - 1; i >= 0; i--) {   // BOTTOM disc first so each one overlaps the one beneath — a stack, not a ladder
        const cy = 11 - i * step;
        disc(cy, 15.5, 5.4, 'rgba(46,28,2,0.9)');    // the keyline: the same disc, larger, under
        disc(cy - 0.7, 13.6, 4.3, '#e0a316');        // the coin's rim
        disc(cy - 1.5, 13.6, 3.4, '#ffcf3d');        // its lit face
        disc(cy - 2.3, 8.2, 1.7, 'rgba(255,246,205,0.75)');   // and the highlight, so a disc reads as ROUND and not as a bar
      }
    },
    take: (p) => {
      pactArm('gtouch');
      if (annAllow('REWARD', 'prz-gtouch')) floaters.push({ lane: 'announce', pr: 2, x: p.x, y: p.y - 130, text: 'GOLDEN TOUCH', age: 0, big: true, color: '#ffcf3d' });
      // NO NEW COLLECT VOICE, and that is the order's own shape rather than a saving: #107 asks for "the
      // chime family an octave up while it runs", so the power announces itself in the sound of the money
      // it doubles — every banked piece, for twelve seconds — instead of in one sting at the pickup. #113
      // owns the per-type stings if the director wants one here too.
      // #113 RULED THAT IT DOES, and the octave chime stays: `SFX.gem(6)` was a BORROWED voice standing in for
      // an arrival, and the twelve seconds of octave-up money it borrowed from is the actual reward. So the cue
      // is now its own (`przGtouch`, above 880Hz where the chime family never goes) and the chime is dropped
      // from the pickup — one banked gem's worth of sound was never what this pickup meant.
      SFX.przGtouch(p.x);
    },
  },
  // ── HEART DROP (#108). A HEART CUT AS A GEM, and the choice is the vocabulary rather than the subject:
  // this game already speaks in cut stones — the diamond, the ruby, the emerald — so a heart that shares
  // their grammar reads as TREASURE a pilot wants, where a flat cartoon heart reads as a UI icon that
  // wandered into the world. Deep ruby body, one bright facet, a dark keyline.
  //   · THE FIFTH SILHOUETTE, and it is unlike the other four on purpose: wide flat trapezoid (gold),
  //     angular diagonal cross (rapid), radial flake (freeze), vertical column (gtouch) — and this one is
  //     LOBED and bottom-pointed. Five kinds now share the air and hue is the first thing a busy frame
  //     takes away.
  //   · Filled polygons with the lobes walked as arcs of points, so the draw recorder can see it (D128).
  heart: {
    key: 'heart', label: 'HEART DROP', tint: '#ff3f5e',
    body: (g, s) => {
      // THE OUTLINE IS THE PARAMETRIC HEART, AND THE FIRST CUT IS WHY. Two hand-tuned lobe arcs stitched at
      // a notch photographed LOPSIDED — a fat left lobe, a bite out of the right — because the two arcs'
      // start angles and sweeps did not actually meet. `x = 16sin³t`, `y = 13cos t − 5cos2t − 2cos3t − cos4t`
      // is SYMMETRIC BY CONSTRUCTION, needs no stitching, and gives the notch and the point for free. The
      // curve is the standard one; what is chosen here is only its scale.
      const ring = (k) => {
        const pts = [];
        for (let i = 0; i < 26; i++) {
          const t = (i / 26) * Math.PI * 2;
          const x = 16 * Math.pow(Math.sin(t), 3);
          const y = -(13 * Math.cos(t) - 5 * Math.cos(2 * t) - 2 * Math.cos(3 * t) - Math.cos(4 * t));
          pts.push([x * k * 0.95, y * k * 0.95]);
        }
        return pts;
      };
      const paint = (k, col) => {
        const pts = ring(k);
        g.fillStyle = col;
        g.beginPath();
        for (let i = 0; i < pts.length; i++) { const q = pts[i]; if (i === 0) g.moveTo(q[0] * s, q[1] * s); else g.lineTo(q[0] * s, q[1] * s); }
        g.closePath(); g.fill();
      };
      paint(1.16, 'rgba(44,4,14,0.9)');   // the keyline: the same silhouette, larger, under — a red mark on warm sand needs it as much as a gold one
      paint(1.0, '#c01834');              // the stone's body, deep
      paint(0.72, '#ff3f5e');             // the lit half of the cut
      // and one hard facet highlight, so it reads CUT rather than painted
      g.fillStyle = 'rgba(255,214,224,0.9)';
      g.beginPath();
      g.moveTo(-7.6 * s, -8.6 * s); g.lineTo(-2.6 * s, -11.0 * s); g.lineTo(-3.4 * s, -4.6 * s); g.lineTo(-8.6 * s, -2.6 * s);
      g.closePath(); g.fill();
    },
    take: (p) => {
      const r = heartDropGrant();
      if (annAllow('REWARD', 'prz-heart')) floaters.push({ lane: 'announce', pr: 3, x: p.x, y: p.y - 130, age: 0, big: true, color: '#ff8fa2', kinetic: 2, text: r.grew ? '+1 MAX HEART  ♥ ' + r.now : 'FULL GROWN ♥ ' + r.now });
      for (let k = 0; k < 16; k++) spawnParticle(p.x, p.y - 8, { vy: -70, spread: 260, life: 0.55, r: 2.8, color: k % 2 ? '#ff8fa2' : '#ffd7e0' });
      SFX.przHeart();
    },
  },
  // ── TIME SLOW (#109). AN HOURGLASS, and it is the vocabulary rather than a flourish: no other object in
  // this game has a PINCHED WAIST, and the waist is the whole reason the shape survives beside the coin
  // stack — both are vertical, and the column is uniform where this one narrows. Violet body per the order,
  // with the sand PALE GOLD so the object reads as treasure a pilot wants as well as a clock.
  //   · THE SIXTH SILHOUETTE, and the set it has to stay legible against is now five: wide flat trapezoid
  //     (gold), angular diagonal cross (rapid), radial flake (freeze), vertical column (gtouch), lobed
  //     bottom-pointed heart (heart). Hue is the first thing a busy frame takes away.
  //   · Filled polygons, points pre-rotated in JS where they need rotating: `harness/env.js` tracks
  //     `translate`/`scale`/`setTransform` and NOT `rotate`, and does not log `stroke()` or `ellipse` at all
  //     (D128 plus ROOM 23's own finding), so a stroked vessel would turn in a browser and be invisible to
  //     every law in the building. Fourth stone running on that constraint.
  slow: {
    key: 'slow', label: 'TIME SLOW', tint: '#b98cff',
    body: (g, s) => {
      // THE VESSEL IS TWO TRAPEZOIDS, NOT TWO TRIANGLES, and that is BAR 1's own distinction: triangles
      // meeting at a point are a BOWTIE, which has no vessel and holds nothing. Flat caps at top and bottom
      // and a waist of real width give it glass to be sand inside of.
      const bulb = (k, col, flip) => {
        const w = 15 * k, wa = 2.6 * k, hy = 15 * k;
        g.fillStyle = col;
        g.beginPath();
        g.moveTo(-w * s, flip * hy * s); g.lineTo(w * s, flip * hy * s);
        g.lineTo(wa * s, 0); g.lineTo(-wa * s, 0);
        g.closePath(); g.fill();
      };
      const caps = (k, col) => {   // the frame: the two end plates a real hourglass is held by
        const w = 17 * k, hy = 15 * k, th = 2.6 * k;
        g.fillStyle = col;
        g.fillRect(-w * s, (hy - th * 0.5) * s, w * 2 * s, th * s);
        g.fillRect(-w * s, (-hy - th * 0.5) * s, w * 2 * s, th * s);
      };
      caps(1.16, 'rgba(18,7,38,0.9)'); bulb(1.16, 'rgba(18,7,38,0.9)', 1); bulb(1.16, 'rgba(18,7,38,0.9)', -1);   // the keyline: the same silhouette, larger, under — a violet mark on warm sand needs it exactly as much as a gold one did (#103)
      bulb(1.0, '#7b4fd0', 1); bulb(1.0, '#7b4fd0', -1);                       // the glass, deep
      bulb(0.62, '#b98cff', 1); bulb(0.62, '#b98cff', -1);                     // and its lit inner, so the vessel reads as ROUND rather than as flat plate
      // THE SAND, which is the only thing in the silhouette that says TIME IS RUNNING. Warm against the
      // violet on purpose: two golds are already in the air (bar, coins) and this is a third warm accent
      // INSIDE a cold body, which reads as contents rather than as another gold prize.
      //
      // **AND ITS FIRST CUT PUT BOTH HEAPS IN THE SAME BULB**, which the 4x frame showed at once: the code
      // read y +0.6..+4.0 and y +7.6..+13.4, both POSITIVE, so the "top bulb's heap" was drawn in the
      // bottom one and the top bulb held nothing at all — a comment saying *drained* over a body that had
      // never been filled. This axis is y-DOWN, the top bulb is the NEGATIVE half, and that one sign is the
      // whole defect. It is #108's `[HD3]` in paint rather than in sound: a comment is not a witness, and
      // the only thing that convicted it was looking.
      // AND ITS SECOND CUT WAS TOO PALE AND TOO SMALL, which is `PATTERNS.md` (3) for the fifth time in this
      // tree and the same lesson every time: a mark is graded against the backdrop it actually lies on, and
      // this one lies on `#b98cff`, not on sand. `#ffd98a` on light violet read as another highlight rather
      // than as contents. A real AMBER separates from violet by hue AND by value, and the heaps are now big
      // enough to be shapes instead of specks.
      g.fillStyle = '#e8a52c';
      g.beginPath();   // the TOP bulb, part drained: a funnel of grain following the wall down to the throat
      g.moveTo(-8.2 * s, -6.6 * s); g.lineTo(8.2 * s, -6.6 * s); g.lineTo(2.3 * s, -0.4 * s); g.lineTo(-2.3 * s, -0.4 * s);
      g.closePath(); g.fill();
      g.beginPath();   // ...and the BOTTOM bulb, risen: a mound sitting on the base plate
      g.moveTo(-12.4 * s, 14.2 * s); g.lineTo(12.4 * s, 14.2 * s); g.lineTo(4.0 * s, 5.6 * s); g.lineTo(-4.0 * s, 5.6 * s);
      g.closePath(); g.fill();
      g.fillStyle = '#ffe0a0';
      g.fillRect(-1.1 * s, -0.4 * s, 2.2 * s, 6.2 * s);   // the falling stream through the waist — the grain in flight, lit brighter than the heaps because it is the only MOVING part of a clock this small
      g.fillStyle = 'rgba(233,220,255,0.9)';              // one hard facet on the upper bulb, so it reads as GLASS
      g.beginPath();
      g.moveTo(-9.6 * s, -11.4 * s); g.lineTo(-4.4 * s, -12.6 * s); g.lineTo(-2.6 * s, -5.2 * s); g.lineTo(-7.0 * s, -4.0 * s);
      g.closePath(); g.fill();
    },
    take: (p) => {
      pactArm('slow');
      if (annAllow('REWARD', 'prz-slow')) floaters.push({ lane: 'announce', pr: 2, x: p.x, y: p.y - 130, text: 'TIME SLOW', age: 0, big: true, color: '#b98cff' });
      SFX.przSlow(p.x);
    },
  },
  over: {
    key: 'over', label: 'OVERDRIVE', tint: '#ff5a1e',
    // THE SEVENTH SILHOUETTE IS A **DOUBLE CHEVRON**, and the choice was made by elimination against the six
    // already in the air rather than by taste: wide flat trapezoid (gold) · crossed diagonal bolts (rapid) ·
    // radial barbed flake (freeze) · stacked disc (gtouch) · lobed bottom-pointed heart (heart) · vertical
    // column with a waist (slow). Two stacked arrowheads pointing UP is none of them, it says MORE without a
    // glyph or a numeral, and its nearest neighbour is the one to beat: the rapid bolt. **A chevron whose arms
    // are too steep, or whose two ranks touch, becomes a kinked cross** — which is BAR 1's own named failure
    // and the reason the ranks are drawn with real sky between them and the arms are shallow (rise 0.62 of
    // half-width, not 1.0).
    //
    // ── "RED-HOT" IS A HEAT GRADIENT, NOT A RED FILL, WHICH IS THE WHOLE OF BAR 2. `heart` already owns
    // pink-red (#ff3f5e) and `rapid` owns amber (#ffb648), so a flat red body would have been the third
    // warm prize in the air and confusable with both at 26px. Hot iron is coolest at its mass and brightest
    // at its thin edges, so this body has FOUR rungs from the outside in — near-black iron keyline, a deep
    // forge-red mass, a brighter orange face, and a pale hot LIP on each chevron's upper edge only. The lip
    // being on the top faces alone is what makes it read as heat rather than as an outline: an even glow all
    // the way round is a keyline, and this file has five of those already.
    //
    // ── EVERY POINT IS PRE-ROTATED IN JS BY CONSTRUCTION — there is no `g.rotate()` here at all, because
    // `harness/env.js` tracks `translate`/`scale`/`setTransform` and IGNORES `rotate`, so a rotated shape
    // turns in a browser and NOT in the recorder. #105's bolts, #106's flake, #108's heart and #109's
    // hourglass all pay this tax; a chevron is drawn from explicit vertices and simply never owes it.
    body: (g, s) => {
      // one rank: an arrowhead with a flat notched underside, so two of them stack without merging.
      const rank = (cy, k, col) => {
        const hw = 15 * k, rise = 9.3 * k, th = 5.4 * k;
        g.fillStyle = col;
        g.beginPath();
        g.moveTo(0, (cy - rise) * s);                       // the apex
        g.lineTo(hw * s, cy * s);                           // right shoulder
        g.lineTo(hw * s, (cy + th * 0.62) * s);             // right tail, thickened
        g.lineTo(0, (cy - rise + th) * s);                  // the inner notch
        g.lineTo(-hw * s, (cy + th * 0.62) * s);
        g.lineTo(-hw * s, cy * s);
        g.closePath(); g.fill();
      };
      // the hot LIP: a thin wedge riding each rank's UPPER faces only — the edge, where thin iron glows.
      //
      // **AND THE FIRST CUT FAILED BAR 2 ON THE BAR'S OWN WORDS, WHICH IS WHY THEY WERE WRITTEN FIRST.**
      // The direction says: *"FAIL if the hot edge is a thin outline all the way round — an outline is a
      // keyline, not heat."* The lip was drawn at the MASS's scale (k 1.00) while the orange face sat at
      // 0.74, so the pale band landed BETWEEN the two and the 7× crop photographed a **chevron badge with
      // white piping** — black, white, orange, white — a military rank insignia, and the deep forge-red mass
      // was a sliver nobody could see. The lip now rides the FACE's own scale with a third of the thickness,
      // so it lies INSIDE the orange as a highlight on the top edge instead of outlining it, and the mass
      // gets a real visible band. `PATTERNS.md` (3) again: the mark was tuned in arithmetic and graded in a
      // photograph, and only the photograph could tell heat from trim.
      const lip = (cy, k, col) => {
        const hw = 15 * k, rise = 9.3 * k, t2 = 1.15 * k;
        g.fillStyle = col;
        g.beginPath();
        g.moveTo(0, (cy - rise) * s);
        g.lineTo(hw * s, cy * s);
        g.lineTo(hw * s, (cy + t2) * s);
        g.lineTo(0, (cy - rise + t2 * 1.7) * s);
        g.lineTo(-hw * s, (cy + t2) * s);
        g.lineTo(-hw * s, cy * s);
        g.closePath(); g.fill();
      };
      // THE KEYLINE, larger and under: the same silhouette in cold iron, so the body holds over pale sand
      // AND over a muzzle flash. `PATTERNS.md` (3)'s cure, and #109's reservation is why it is dark iron
      // rather than a warm outline — this body is graded on the DESERT, which is warm.
      rank(-2.0, 1.20, 'rgba(24,7,4,0.92)'); rank(11.0, 1.34, 'rgba(24,7,4,0.92)');
      rank(-2.0, 1.00, '#8c1f08'); rank(11.0, 1.14, '#8c1f08');        // the mass: forge-red, the coolest iron — and it must be a visible BAND, not a sliver
      rank(-2.0, 0.80, '#ff5a1e'); rank(11.0, 0.94, '#ff5a1e');        // the face: hot orange
      lip(-2.0, 0.80, '#ffd9a0'); lip(11.0, 0.94, '#ffd9a0');          // and the LIP: pale, at the FACE's scale so it is a highlight ON the orange, never a band between the orange and the mass
    },
    take: (p) => {
      pactArm('over');
      if (annAllow('REWARD', 'prz-over')) floaters.push({ lane: 'announce', pr: 2, x: p.x, y: p.y - 130, text: 'OVERDRIVE', age: 0, big: true, color: '#ff5a1e' });
      SFX.przOver(p.x);
    },
  },
  decoy: {
    key: 'decoy', label: 'THE DECOY', tint: '#9dffc4',
    // THE EIGHTH SILHOUETTE IS A STANDING FIGURE IN A FRAME, and the elimination is the same as #110's: none
    // of the seven in the air is a HUMAN outline. A tiny pale figure inside a bracket says *another one of
    // you* without a glyph, and the bracket is what keeps it from reading as a real body lying in the sand -
    // a prize is an object, and an object holding a person is a projector.
    body: (g, s) => {
      const fig = (k, col) => {   // the little man: head, shoulders, legs. Blocked, not drawn - 22px of body.
        g.fillStyle = col;
        g.beginPath(); g.arc(0, -9.6 * k * s, 3.5 * k * s, 0, Math.PI * 2); g.fill();   // the head
        g.fillRect(-4.6 * k * s, -5.6 * k * s, 9.2 * k * s, 8.4 * k * s);               // the torso
        g.fillRect(-4.2 * k * s, 2.8 * k * s, 3.1 * k * s, 7.2 * k * s);                // and two legs, apart, so it reads as STANDING
        g.fillRect(1.1 * k * s, 2.8 * k * s, 3.1 * k * s, 7.2 * k * s);
      };
      // THE BRACKET: two corner pairs, top and bottom, the frame a projection is thrown inside. Filled
      // rectangles only - `harness/env.js` sees `fill()` and not `stroke()`, so a stroked frame would exist
      // in a browser and in no law at all. #105's bolts learned this and every body since has paid it.
      const bracket = (k, col) => {
        const w = 12.6 * k, h = 14.4 * k, t = 2.2 * k, len = 5.4 * k;
        g.fillStyle = col;
        for (const sx of [-1, 1]) for (const sy of [-1, 1]) {
          g.fillRect((sx > 0 ? w - len : -w) * s, (sy > 0 ? h - t : -h) * s, len * s, t * s);        // the horizontal arm
          g.fillRect((sx > 0 ? w - t : -w) * s, (sy > 0 ? h - len : -h) * s, t * s, len * s);        // and the vertical
        }
      };
      bracket(1.20, 'rgba(6,26,20,0.9)'); fig(1.28, 'rgba(6,26,20,0.9)');   // the keyline, larger and under: over sand, over sky and over a muzzle flash
      bracket(1.00, '#3fbf8c');                                             // the frame, deep green
      fig(1.00, '#9dffc4');                                                 // and the figure, pale mint - the brightest thing in the body, because it IS the body
    },
    take: (p) => {
      pactArm('decoy');
      if (annAllow('REWARD', 'prz-decoy')) floaters.push({ lane: 'announce', pr: 2, x: p.x, y: p.y - 130, text: 'THE DECOY', age: 0, big: true, color: '#9dffc4' });
      // THE DROP POINT IS THE ORDER'S OWN WORD, and it is the PRIZE's x rather than the pilot's: a decoy that
      // posted where he stands would be a lie about where he is, which is the opposite of the mechanic.
      decoyPost(p.x, groundY(), player.facing || 1);
      SFX.przDecoy(p.x);
    },
  },
  // ── ENDLESS AMMO (G6). HIS ORDER: "your rockets never run out of ammo for 10 seconds should be a
  // gem shmoopy dropes." HIS RULING: from shmoopys AND the ordinary rotation, visible timer. The
  // NINTH silhouette is a rocket shell leaning into flight — none of the eight in the air is a
  // projectile outline. Chartreuse, because the eight own gold/amber/ice/red/purple/orange/mint and
  // the sun's pale-gold halo needs clearing (the ΔE census below grades it). Filled quads only, points
  // pre-rotated in JS — `harness/env.js` sees `fill()` and not `rotate()`, the family's own lesson.
  ammo: {
    key: 'ammo', label: 'ENDLESS AMMO', tint: '#c8e34a',
    body: (g, s) => {
      const SHELL = [
        [[-3.5, -8], [3.5, -8], [0, -15]],                 // the nose cone
        [[-3.5, -8], [3.5, -8], [3.5, 8], [-3.5, 8]],      // the body tube
        [[-3.5, 5], [-8.5, 14], [-3.5, 13]],               // the left fin
        [[3.5, 5], [8.5, 14], [3.5, 13]],                  // and the right
      ];
      const rot = 0.62;   // leaning into flight — an upright shell reads as a bottle
      const put = (col, sc) => {
        const c = Math.cos(rot), sn = Math.sin(rot);
        g.fillStyle = col;
        for (const poly of SHELL) {
          g.beginPath();
          for (let i = 0; i < poly.length; i++) {
            const x = poly[i][0] * sc, y = poly[i][1] * sc;
            const rx = (x * c - y * sn) * s, ry = (x * sn + y * c) * s;
            if (i === 0) g.moveTo(rx, ry); else g.lineTo(rx, ry);
          }
          g.closePath(); g.fill();
        }
      };
      put('rgba(24, 30, 4, 0.9)', 1.24);   // the dark keyline, larger and under — #104's lesson, a warm mark on warm sand
      put('#9db32c', 1.06);                // the shell, one tone down
      put('#c8e34a', 0.88);                // the lit face, full chartreuse over it
      const c2 = Math.cos(rot), s2 = Math.sin(rot);   // the hot core: a pale stripe up the tube — lit from inside, the family idiom
      g.fillStyle = 'rgba(246, 255, 214, 0.85)';
      g.beginPath();
      for (const [px9, py9] of [[-1.1, -10], [1.1, -10], [1.1, 6], [-1.1, 6]]) {
        const rx = (px9 * c2 - py9 * s2) * s, ry = (px9 * s2 + py9 * c2) * s;
        if (px9 === -1.1 && py9 === -10) g.moveTo(rx, ry); else g.lineTo(rx, ry);
      }
      g.closePath(); g.fill();
    },
    take: (p) => {
      pactArm('ammo');
      if (annAllow('REWARD', 'prz-ammo')) floaters.push({ lane: 'announce', pr: 2, x: p.x, y: p.y - 130, text: 'ENDLESS AMMO', age: 0, big: true, color: '#c8e34a' });
      SFX.przAmmo(p.x);
    },
  },
};
// THE THIRD STREAM'S HANDLE, declared HERE rather than beside the scheduler because `prizeSpawn`
// below is its first reader and a stream must exist before the thing that drinks from it. It is
// re-seeded once per wave by `psPlan()`, and the whole argument for why THE BOUNCE has its own dice
// at all is in THE SPAWN LAW's block comment further down. `mulberry32` is this file's own generator
// — the one the seed lock and wavesim [G9] already use — found by SEARCH-BEFORE-ARGUMENT rather than
// a second PRNG being written. On the DEV DESK it is never re-seeded, so the hand crank deals the
// same sequence every session: harmless, and a bench that repeats is a bench you can compare on.
let przRand = mulberry32(1);
// THE ONE PREDICATE — read by the tick AND by the draw, never duplicated.
function prizeStage() { return SANDBOX.on || tutorial.active || (gameMode === 'wave' && WRUN.on && !WMODE.screen); }   // tutorial.active: THE CATCH is lesson 15 (his spec) — the classroom hosts one staged prize; endTutorial clears the field like any door change
// the dials reach the DEV DESK only: the wave run rides the shipped table (the no-cheat law). Said in
// every caption too, because ROOM 20 lost a murder to a dial that silently did not reach wave mode.
// 0 on a dial means PINNED — ride the shipped number — which is this file's own convention on every
// tuning page. So a bare desk changes nothing, and only a turned dial speaks.
function przD(k) { return SANDBOX.on && SANDBOX.prize && SANDBOX.prize[k] > 0 ? SANDBOX.prize[k] : PRIZE[k.toUpperCase()]; }
// THE POWER PAGE'S THREE READERS (#112), and each one is a CHOKEPOINT rather than a sprinkle — criterion C
// says every dial must MOVE something, proved at its effect, and a reader with one call site is a claim a law
// can check by grep as well as by behaviour.
//   · `pkDur(kind)` — read by `pactArm`, the ONE door that arms a slot. Every kind's duration passes here.
//   · `pkWt(R)`     — read TWICE inside `psRoll`, in the total and in the walk, because a weighted pick that
//                     dialed one and not the other would deal a kind at a probability neither number states.
//   · `pkSkip(k)`   — read by `psRoll`'s own `open` filter, beside the `!PRIZE_KINDS[R.k]` clause that already
//                     drops unbuilt rows. A suppressed kind is *exactly* an unbuilt one for the roll's purpose.
// **AND ALL THREE ARE DESK-ONLY BY CONSTRUCTION, WHICH IS CRITERION B.** `SANDBOX.on` gates every one, and 0
// rides the table — so THE NO-CHEAT LAW holds without a second check: a wave run cannot reach these dials, and
// a bare desk changes nothing. That is `przD`'s shape, copied rather than re-invented (SEARCH-BEFORE-ARGUMENT).
function pkDur(kind) { return SANDBOX.on && SANDBOX.pkDur && SANDBOX.pkDur[kind] > 0 ? SANDBOX.pkDur[kind] : PACT_DUR[kind]; }
function pkWt(R) { return SANDBOX.on && SANDBOX.pkWt && SANDBOX.pkWt[R.k] > 0 ? SANDBOX.pkWt[R.k] : R.wt; }
function pkSkip(k) { return !!(SANDBOX.on && SANDBOX.pkSkip && SANDBOX.pkSkip[k]); }
// AND THE ENTRY FLIP DRINKS FROM `przRand`, NOT FROM THE WAR — #100's own finding, and it is #78's
// doctrine APPLIED rather than invented. `spawnParticle`'s own comment states the test: "a spark thrown
// by a bullet is caused by the war and belongs to its determinism. A DIALED system does not: the moment
// its dial can be turned down, drinking from a shared stream means the dial re-rolls somebody else's
// world." THE BOUNCE has eleven dials, so it is the second system in this file to pass its own dice.
// WHY IT MATTERS ONLY NOW: at #99 the sole caller was a hand crank on the dev desk, so this flip was
