  //   to 14% of its width edge-on and opens back out; at that extreme the girdle line is most of what is
  //   left and it reads as a bright sliver, which is exactly what a real stone does in a spotlight.
  //   Baked into the COORDINATES rather than set as a transform, so the strokes stay round: a non-uniform
  //   ctx.scale would squash every lineWidth with the body and the hard edge would go soft exactly edge-on.
  //   ⚠ THE FLOOR WAS 0.14 IN THE FIRST CUT AND THE INSPECTION FRAME REFUTED THE DIRECTION'S OWN WORDS.
  //   At 14% a 21px stone is a THREE-PIXEL-WIDE dark green needle - it reads as debris, not as a stone
  //   turning, and DIRECTION.md section 5 had promised "narrows to a bright SLIVER". 0.30 is the widest
  //   value that still clears C3's 45% bar with margin, and at 6px across the facets survive the turn.
  const sq = 0.30 + 0.70 * Math.abs(Math.cos(spinPhase));
  // ── THE STROKES SQUASH TOO (#339, SECOND PASS — the cold reader's shortfall #7, deferred by the first
  //   pass as "the next stone's" and cured here because it is three characters of arithmetic). **A stroke
  //   is drawn in SCREEN space, so a constant `lineWidth` does not turn with the stone**: the body narrows
  //   to 38.5% of full width and the DRAWN silhouette only to ~49%, because a 1.2px contour and a 1.6px
  //   fire stroke each add their full width at both extremes. The promise in DIRECTION.md section 5 is
  //   "narrows to a bright SLIVER", and a sliver wearing a full-width outline is a sliver with a border.
  //   0.45 + 0.55·sq, NOT sq itself: a contour that scaled linearly would be 0.36px edge-on, which the
  //   rasteriser turns into a grey smear rather than a line, and the dark contour is `JEWELRY-STANDARD.md`
  //   1a's own cure for a gem reading as a sticker — it may thin, never vanish. The GIRDLE is deliberately
  //   NOT scaled: it does the opposite by ruling (it burns hardest edge-on), and that asymmetry is the
  //   whole read — at full squash the outline steps back and the girdle is what is left.
  const sw = 0.45 + 0.55 * sq;
  // ── THE PITCH (BOOK II #339). **THE FIRST CUT HAD NONE AND A COLD READER CAUGHT IT:** `py` was
  //   `v[1] * rr`, unscaled, while `px` carried the whole squash — *"every one of the 34 stones I measured
  //   is a SIDE VIEW; the girdle is always a straight bar and never projects as an ellipse, and the table is
  //   never seen face-on."* That is the difference between a body of revolution and a card, and it is why a
  //   few frames read as a slash ACROSS the stone rather than as an edge.
  //   TWO TERMS, and they are the whole of it. `fore` foreshortens the profile as the stone tips away from
  //   the equator. `bow` displaces every horizontal ring's NEAR side — the girdle, the table edge, every
  //   facet boundary — by `1 - x²`, which is exactly an ellipse's front-half displacement in unit space.
  //   **Applying it as a pure function of x is what keeps the six facets TILING**: a vertex shared by two
  //   facets moves identically in both, so no seam can open however far the stone tips.
  //   ⚠ **IT IS A CHEAP PROJECTION AND NOT A TRUE ONE, said plainly:** there is no perspective divide and no
  //   real rotation matrix, so the far half of the girdle is never drawn and a stone can never be seen from
  //   directly above. At a 21px body that is invisible; at trophy scale it would not be. The pitch clock is
