    //   measured the crown as the brighter half in 20 of 22 frames, so the flash was travelling ACROSS the
    //   stone and never DOWN it. Antiphase gives the pavilion - 62% of the body's area - its own half of
    //   every cycle, which is also the honest physics: light enters the crown and leaves the pavilion.
    //   ⚠ THE FIRST CUT OF THE BLINK WEIGHTED THE LIFT BY `lit` AND THE MEASUREMENT REFUSED IT: zero-near-
    //   white moved 80.0% -> 81.5%, i.e. nowhere. **A lit-weighted blink is not a blink, it is the same
    //   one-facet gradient turned up** - at full blink a DARK facet only reached 33% lightness, so the stone
    //   still had no white in it. This blends every facet toward the ceiling instead: at the peak the dark
    //   pavilion goes to ~84% and the lit crown to ~96%, which is a stone that briefly owns its silhouette.
    //   That is the whole difference between "brighter where it was already bright" and a FLASH.
    //   AND IT FIRES TWICE PER TURN, NOT ONCE - `|cos|`, not `max(0, cos)`. At one narrow lobe per cycle
    //   only about a tenth of a volley is flashing at any instant, and the measurement said so: zero-near-
    //   white fell only 67.9% -> 62.1%. **The cure for that is MORE flashes, never longer ones** - a longer
    //   lobe stops being a blink and becomes the steady lamp this whole stone exists to kill. Two lobes is
