// klaxon's ladder, the swell voice cut short for THE CARRIER-SILENCE, the pooled blast.
// ══ THE FAMILY HAS THREE VOICES (BOOK II #383) — AND THE LOCK WAS NEVER SILENT ═══════════════════════════
// **THE ORDER SAYS THE LOCK IS SILENT FOR EVERY TIER. IT IS NOT, AND THE PRIOR-ART SEARCH IS WHAT SAID SO.**
// The lock already carries THREE layers: this entry chirp, `SUIWHINE` (*"the one throat all locked grudges
// share"*, rising in pitch AND volume with proximity, nearest body only), and `SFX.suiKlaxon`'s jaws clock
// quickening as it closes. **The real defect is that all three are TIER-DEAF** — a gold grudge, whose
// reaction window is the one that halves, sounds exactly like a common one. That is the gap worth closing,
// and it is a smaller and better-aimed change than inventing a lock voice on top of three.
//
// ⚠ **THE TIER RIDES RHYTHM, NOT PITCH, AND THAT IS THE STONE'S ONE REAL DERIVATION.** Pitch is already
// spoken for: the whine maps it to PROXIMITY and the klaxon maps its rate to the same thing. Hanging tier on
// pitch too would put two variables on one dimension and a pilot would read neither. **Rhythm is free, and
// it is the channel that survives a pilot whose eyes are elsewhere** — which is this stone's whole bar.
// So: the common keeps its shipped two beats EXACTLY (he named this noise as one he likes), and the gold
// says the same thing THREE times with a low partner under it. Two-versus-three is countable without
// looking; a semitone is not.
SFX.suiLock = (hx, gold) => {
  // The shipped pair, byte-identical — 1900->2400 square, 0.09s, 0.07, the second at +0.14s.
  tone({ f0: 1900, f1: 2400, dur: 0.09, type: 'square', vol: 0.07, x: hx, cue: 'tele' });   // (RED-2) a lock is a telegraph: it plays under red
  tone({ f0: 1900, f1: 2400, dur: 0.09, type: 'square', vol: 0.07, delay: 0.14, x: hx, cue: 'tele' });
  if (!gold) return;
  tone({ f0: 1900, f1: 2400, dur: 0.09, type: 'square', vol: 0.07, delay: 0.28, x: hx, cue: 'tele' });   // the third beat
  // ...and a fifth below the whine's own floor, so the gold lock has a FLOOR the common one does not. A
  // fifth is 2/3 the frequency: it thickens rather than detunes, which a fourth or a tritone would.
  tone({ f0: 466, f1: 622, dur: 0.34, type: 'triangle', vol: 0.055, x: hx, cue: 'tele' });
};
// ══ THE SUPER'S PRESENCE — AND IT IS `rbPresence`'s SIBLING, NOT ITS COPY ════════════════════════════════
// **THE ORDER RULES THIS ONE BY NAME: *"a low continuous presence — something you notice has been under the
// mix for a while — says that better than an event does."*** The super never locks and never sprints, so an
// event voice would be a lie about a body whose whole identity is that it does not change its mind.
// **THE GRAMMAR IS ALREADY IN THIS FILE.** `SFX.rbPresence` is a 90->84Hz sine at 0.022, and its comment
// records that it is **deliberately un-positioned — no `x` — "because it is the LINE's presence and not one
// man's."** That sentence is exactly why this one IS positioned: **a super is one body, and where it is
// coming from is the information.** Same idea, opposite answer, for a stated reason.
// **AND ITS PITCH IS THE FAMILY'S OWN.** #381 dropped the super's boom chest to 62->19Hz. Rooting the
// presence at 62 makes it the same throat at rest — the body you have been hearing is the body that goes
// off — rather than a new number chosen to sound low.
SFX.suiPresence = (hx) => tone({ f0: 62, f1: 58, dur: 0.62, type: 'sine', vol: 0.026, x: hx });
SFX.suiKlaxon = (near20, hx) => tone({ f0: 1750 + near20 * 700, f1: 2300 + near20 * 700, dur: 0.07, type: 'square', vol: 0.05 + near20 * 0.045, x: hx });
SFX.suiSwell = (hx) => tone({ f0: 300, f1: 900, dur: 0.28, type: 'sawtooth', vol: 0.08, x: hx });   // THE CARRIER-SILENCE (#20): 0.28s of voice, then ~0.2s of NOTHING before the boom — the dropped signal IS the tell
SFX.suiBoom = (hx, big) => {
  // ⚠ **THE BIGGER THROAT IS PITCH AND LENGTH, NEVER LEVEL (#381), AND `audiosim [A2]` IS WHY.** The order
  //   owes the super a bigger voice; the 0.2 ceiling above says it cannot be a louder one, and that ceiling
  //   caught this file's own first cut at 0.5. **The constraint turns out to be the correct physics anyway:
  //   a bigger detonation is not a louder crack, it is a LOWER and LONGER one.** A small charge snaps; a
  //   large one drops into your chest and keeps going. So `big` moves the chest sub from 90->28 Hz down to
  //   62->19, stretches the body and the hiss, and **leaves every `vol` byte-identical** — the weight comes
  //   from the sub sitting nearly an octave lower for a third again as long. Held by `[SBM]`.
  const bigT = big ? 1.35 : 1, bigF = big ? 0.69 : 1;
  if (suiBoomStamp !== gameTime) { suiBoomStamp = gameTime; suiBoomN = 0; }
  if (++suiBoomN > 3) return;   // THE POOL (#20): a rain of detonations is three booms a frame, not thirty (#52 will lean on this)
  // (#319) **THE VOICE WAS 3.3 dB QUIETER AT PEAK THAN `SFX.boom` — the ORDINARY explosion — after three
  //   stones tripled its picture.** Measured, not felt: suiBoom -24.29 / boom -20.99, and the spectrogram
  //   showed the real defect, which loudness alone would have hidden: **no vertical transient at t=0.** The
  //   energy faded IN, smeared, and sagged out over half a second. The honest description of the shipped
  //   sound was *a muffled buzzy motor stalling*, against a blast that now throws a 6x flash, debris, a
  //   ground light and a scorch mark. THE THREE PARTS, and the old voice is the middle one UNCHANGED:
  //   1. THE CRACK — a hard bright noise transient, 6 hundredths long. This is the vertical stripe, and it
  //      is what makes a detonation read as a detonation rather than as a swell.
  //   2. THE BODY — the shipped 180->40 sawtooth and its 3000->300 hiss, kept exactly, because the family
  //      keeps its voice (`notes/FORGE-SOUND.md`: join a family, never invent one).
  //   **AND EVERY LEVEL HERE OBEYS `audiosim [A2]`'s 0.2 CEILING, which caught my first cut at 0.5 and
  //   0.34 and was right to.** That ceiling protects the master limiter (-16 threshold, ratio 5) and it
  //   exempts exactly one voice, `SFX.boom`, pinned so it cannot creep. **I did not ask for a second
  //   exemption.** The weight comes from FOUR legal throats instead of two loud ones — 0.2 + 0.13 + 0.11 +
  //   0.2 against the flagship boom's 0.26 + 0.22 — which is a fuller sound than one throat shouting, and
  //   it leaves the limiter's headroom law untouched.
  //   3. THE CHEST — a sine sub falling 90->28 Hz. The old low end was a SAWTOOTH, which is a buzz with
  //      harmonics all the way up; a detonation wants a body under the crack, and a sine is that body.
  noise({ dur: 0.06, vol: 0.2, f0: 9000, f1: 1400, x: hx });               // 1. THE CRACK
  tone({ f0: 180 * bigF, f1: 40 * bigF, dur: 0.5 * bigT, type: 'sawtooth', vol: 0.13, x: hx });  // 2. THE BODY, as shipped
  noise({ dur: 0.4 * bigT, vol: 0.11, f0: 3000, f1: 300, x: hx });         // 2b. **`x` ADDED — a plain bug:**
  //      every other throat in this file passes `x` and `noise` applies `spatial(x).att` to it. Without it
  //      this half played at FULL LEVEL no matter where on the field the grudge died — not merely unpanned,
  //      UNATTENUATED, so a detonation across the map was as loud in its hiss as one at your feet.
  tone({ f0: 90 * bigF, f1: 28 * bigF, dur: 0.55 * bigT, type: 'sine', vol: 0.2, x: hx });      // 3. THE CHEST
  // **AND IT PUSHES THE DUCKING TABLE, WHICH IS HOW A BOOM GETS SIZE WITHOUT VOLUME** — the table at
  //   `DUCK.EV` is the declared place for it and the blast was not in its six-event census. **TIER 0 ONLY,
  //   and that cap is the answer to this stone`s named silent risk**: the voice pool already allows three a
  //   frame, and three ducks a frame at wave 10 would hold the bed down permanently and never let it
  //   breathe back. The first blast of a frame ducks; the second and third are heard against the duck the
  //   first one bought. Music is deliberately NOT ducked — `mus: false`, like the bell and the stinger —
  //   because a detonation is common and a beat that flinches thirty-five times a wave is a broken mix.
  if (suiBoomN === 1) bedDuck(DUCK.EV.blast.bed);
};
// **RENAMED, NOT DELETED** (BOOK II #315). Its subject — the defuse — is gone, and THE ORPHAN CLAUSE would
//   let this be retired outright. But the REWARD was not deleted, only moved, and this is exactly the right
//   sound for where it moved to: a bright confirmation that you did the skilful thing. Same voice, honest
//   name, and the sound board keeps its entry rather than growing a hole.
SFX.earlyChime = (hx) => { tone({ f0: 1046, f1: 1046, dur: 0.09, type: 'triangle', vol: 0.09, x: hx }); tone({ f0: 1568, f1: 1568, dur: 0.14, type: 'triangle', vol: 0.08, delay: 0.07, x: hx }); tone({ f0: 2093, f1: 2093, dur: 0.2, type: 'sine', vol: 0.06, delay: 0.14, x: hx }); };   // THE DEFUSE (#19): a rising major arpeggio — victory in three notes, nothing else uses it
SFX.stMirror = () => { tone({ f0: 220, f1: 110, dur: 0.9, type: 'sawtooth', vol: 0.12 }); tone({ f0: 466, f1: 440, dur: 0.7, type: 'triangle', vol: 0.07, delay: 0.2 }); noise({ f0: 200, f1: 60, dur: 0.5, vol: 0.07, delay: 0.1 }); };   // THE ENTRANCE (#28): a dark swell with a semitone-wrong echo — the memory played back damaged (#39 grows the voice family)
// THE DEATH CEREMONY's voice (#126) — "the stinger RESOLVES", and it resolves against a specific wrongness
// rather than in general. The entrance's echo ENTERS at 466 (B♭) and slides down to 440 (A): a memory that
// arrives out of tune and has to correct itself on the way in. This one never enters wrong — it starts ON
// 440, holds it flat, and puts its own octave above it, so the two throats close a clean octave where the
// entrance closed a semitone slide. And the swell falls TWO octaves (220 -> 55) where the entrance fell
// one, because the body is going out of the world rather than into it. The glass rides a noise burst on
// the blow itself. HEARD-PROOF: the spectrogram must SHOW two flat lines at 440 and 880 against the
// entrance's descending smear — a promise about a sound is a promise a picture of it can refute.
// ══ `fgShatter` (BOOK II #166) — **THE FORGE'S OWN DEATH VOICE, AND IT JOINS A FAMILY RATHER THAN
// INVENTING ONE.** `notes/FORGE-SOUND.md` §5 ruled every number in it thirty-six stones ago: the band is
// **−18…−16 dBFS on the `wave` bus**, graded against `stPriest` (−16.63, the loudest single voice in the
// game) and never more than **1.15 dB** above it — the hear rig's own measured reproducibility spread,
// which is also why the doc rules a 2 dB BAND and forbids a pinned decimal: *a band narrower than the
// instrument's noise is a coin flip.* **It is NOT graded against `shbreak`**; the ring's ceiling is the
// ring's, and §5 says so by name. The forge does not become the loudest thing the game says. It joins
// the top of the announce family.
//
// **THE FIRST CUT BORROWED `SFX.shieldBreak` AND THAT WAS A HIT, NOT A CEREMONY** — a short bright crack
// on the `ui` bus fronting 2.6 s of falling glass. This stone's own direction makes that FAIL 5: *the
// voice must have a BODY, or the sound contradicts the picture.* Four throats, and each is a quotation:
//   1. THE CRACK — `stMirrorDie`'s glass burst, brighter and longer, because 300 units break louder
//      than a 110px man.
//   2. THE FLOOR — `sealTear`'s own 46→22 Hz sine, the sound this engine already makes when something
//      huge resolves. **Sub energy is what reads as ENORMOUS; loudness alone only reads as LOUD**, and
//      that distinction is the one question the direction asks the ear to answer.
//   3. THE BODY — `stMirrorDie`'s two-octave fall, deeper (180→40) and longer (1.6 s), because this
//      body is bigger and its ceremony is 2.6 s against the duel's 1.7.
//   4. THE RESOLVE — `sealTear`'s upward triangle, then `stPriest`'s OWN interval in the tail, so the
//      beat ENDS inside the announce family it is graded against instead of merely measuring like it.
// ══ `fgPrice` (BOOK II #169) — **THE LOSS, AND IT IS A SENTENCE WHERE THE WIN IS A BANG.**
// `notes/FORGE-SOUND.md` §5 declares **−28 … −25 dBFS**, *deliberately under the win*, and §9's own
// humility clause admitted the whole family was unrendered: *"IT HAS NOT RENDERED A SINGLE FORGE
// VOICE."* `fgShatter` cured that for the win at #166; this is the other half, and the criterion is
// explicit that **the loss must not sound like a win.**
//
// **IT IS THE WIN'S OWN MATERIAL, PLAYED DOWNWARD.** `fgShatter` opens on a wide-band crack, drops two
// octaves and RESOLVES UPWARD into `stPriest`'s interval. This takes the same throats and refuses the
// resolve: no crack (nothing broke), a shorter floor, a fall that keeps falling, and a closing interval
