// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// THE BLIMP RULING (BOOK II #227, LEG 8) — **A BLIMP IS ARCHITECTURE. IT IS A PLACE, NOT A BODY.**
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// The order offered two answers and neither is right, and the code is what says so rather than taste:
//
//   NOT AN ENEMY. `LAUNCHERS_ENABLED` is false, so `spawnBlimp` never pushes the launcher body. Nothing
//   on a blimp is targetable; `blimpKills` has exactly one writer and it sits behind a launcher's death,
//   which cannot happen; the HUD objective line is wrapped in the same flag and is therefore never shown.
//   The launcher has no `e.fam` tag either — `notes/SCALING-DOCTRINE.md` recorded it as "still anonymous"
//   twice. **A family nothing can shoot, that pays nothing, that the census cannot name, is not a family.**
//
//   NOT FAR LIFE EITHER, AND THIS IS THE HALF THE BOOK MISSED. Far life is `[FL]`'s birds and `[TWD]`'s
//   weeds: never-targetable theater that touches nothing and that the pilot's bolt passes straight
//   through. **YOU CANNOT STAND ON FAR LIFE.** A blimp's gondola is a real `platforms[]` entry with
//   rider-carry, a pulsing landing beacon, and three of the twenty ships carrying vendors who live there.
//   Calling it theater would be a ruling that contradicts the beacon inviting the pilot to land on it.
//
// **SO: A BLIMP IS INHABITED ARCHITECTURE — the only thing in this game that is a PLACE.** Weeds are
// theater, crates are breakable theater, birds are far life, and blimps are somewhere you go.
//
// **THE RULING HAS ONE CONSEQUENCE AND IT IS MADE TRUE IN THE SAME EDIT, not left as prose.** Architecture
// you stand on should be standable wherever it exists. Until #223 the desert emptied the platform array
// every frame, so a deck out here could never catch anyone and "far life" was the only ruling the code
// would support. #223 replaced that with a narrow named rule — `platLandable` refuses a desert platform
// unless it carries `desertLand` — which means the question stopped being architectural and became a
// one-word decision. **The word is set below.** A pilot who deploys a blimp on the desert can now fly up
// and stand on it; before this stone he fell straight through a deck with a beacon on it.
//
// **WHAT THIS RULING DOES NOT DO, said plainly so the next stone does not have to re-derive it:**
