//   band, the layer of SMALL, DIM, SLOW clouds (scale 0.46–0.84, alpha 0.66, parallax 0.15). A blimp is
//   drawn at `BL_SCALE` 0.85, fully opaque, moving on its own throttle: **a large sharp fast object parked
//   in the depth reserved for the smallest and faintest things in the sky.** That is ledger item 25(a)'s
//   class — a body whose depth cues contradict the layer it sits in — and it only became visible at
//   #227/#228, when the fleet started being drawn on the desert at all.
//
// **THE CITATION IN THE ORDER IS WRONG AND ITS FIGURES ARE RIGHT:** there is no `SKYCLOUD.bandD`; the
// bands live on `SKYCLOUD.layers[]`, one pair per depth. Far median 490 / mid 1050 / near 1800 and the
// near floor of 780 all check out exactly against the real rows.
const ALT_BANDS = {
  // dy, measured up from `groundY()`. Every row here is reconciled against `SKYCLOUD.layers[].bandD`.
  desk:   [820, 1180],   // **NEAR.** A deployed ship should be the NEAREST thing in the sky, not the
                         //   furthest. The near band's floor of 780 is itself derived — the standing
                         //   pilot's frame reaches dy 757 — so 820 is the lowest altitude at which a body
                         //   reads as SKY rather than as scenery he could reach.
  armada: [720, 2570],   // unchanged, and recorded here so the reconciliation is readable in one place
  // **THE FORGE BAND IS DECLARED AND NOT WIRED (the order's clause B).** LEG 6's forge has no altitude
  //   band built at this point in the book. This row is the hook it will read, sited inside the near band
  //   so the forge arrives at a depth whose cues match a large bright body. **`[BND]` clause 4 is the
  //   census: it fails if anything in `game.js` reads this row**, because a hook that claims a door
  //   nobody built is `PATTERNS.md` (2) — the guard that announces without acting.
  forge:  [1300, 1900],
};
