//      never looked at. Any atmosphere built inside these fences inherits the law free. ====
// ======================= THE ATMOSPHERE BUDGET (THE-TWO-FIFTY #84) =======================
// THE ORDER, verbatim: "THE ATMOSPHERE BUDGET — the whole desert's systems measured together on the
// honest meter; a printed frame budget pinned; auto-scaler aware."
//
// ---- THREE OF THE FOUR ORGANS THIS STONE NEEDED WERE ALREADY BUILT, and finding them was its first
// act rather than its last (SEARCH-BEFORE-ARGUMENT). THE HONEST METER (ARMORY #3) already times
// update+draw into METER.ms and every tuning page already prints the strip. THE HITCH LEDGER
// (HITCH-HUNT #1) already keeps 240 frames of the same number. AND THE AUTO-SCALER ALREADY EXISTS:
// GAUGE.guard, armed by govGuard — two seconds under 20fps arms it; since v1.6.1419 THE RED LATCH clears it only after GOV.HOLD s and GOV.CLR_S s of the update half under GOV.CLR_MS ms (never on fps), and since v1.6.1423 THE LATCH HOLE only while live bolts and bodies on glass are under GOV.CLR_BOLTS / GOV.CLR_BODIES.
// UNTIL v1.6.679 arming it also cut PBUDGET from 700 to 300; that cut is retired and the guard now
// only reports (see frameInner's own block). So "auto-scaler aware" was never an order to build a scaler; it was an
// order to JOIN one. The finding that follows is the whole reason this block exists: OF THE NINE
// ATMOSPHERE SYSTEMS (notes/ATMOSPHERE-WIRE.md), EXACTLY ONE WAS ALREADY AWARE OF IT — #78's dust,
// whose ceiling is a SHARE of PBUDGET and which therefore gives way on its own the moment the guard
// bites. The other eight kept drawing at full price into a frame that was already drowning.
//
// ---- THE NUMBERS ARE MEASURED, NOT CHOSEN. tools/cost-rig.js boots the SHIPPED artifact in a real
// browser, stages the desert, and times the honest meter's own quantity — CPU wall for update+draw —
// with one system removed at a time, as the median of interleaved pairs. The full table is in
// BUDGET.md and it is re-takeable in one command. THE GATE CANNOT TAKE THIS MEASUREMENT AND NEVER
// COULD: no canvas, no WebP, so the sky and the sand do not exist inside the harness at all
// (notes/SKY-GRADING.md). budgetsim [AB] pins what a headless box CAN see — the path math — and says
// so in its own text rather than implying it measured the picture.
const ATMBUD = {
  acc: 0,           // this frame's atmosphere wall, accumulated pass by pass between atmIn/atmOut
  t0: 0,            // ...the open bracket's stamp
  ms: 0,            // ...smoothed exactly the way METER.ms is smoothed, and printed beside it
  SHED: 0,          // THE DIAL's wire. 1: when the frame is drowning the SKY thins and the fight does
  //                   not. 0: the weather never gives way, whatever happens to the frame.
  //                 THE DEFAULT FLIPPED 1 -> 0 at v1.6.679 (BOOK II #82), by the director's own
  //                 correction: "my problem has been not the auto-throttle but a throttle that
  //                 doesn't let me even go outside and play for a minute." The MECHANISM is
  //                 untouched — every line of `atmShedding`/`atmKeep`/`atmDrawn` still works and the
  //                 WEATHER SHED dial still turns it on. What changed is which way it ships: the
  //                 frame now drops honestly instead of the sky going quietly thin, because a
  //                 pilot who cannot see the edge cannot tell you where it is. One value back.
  CAP: 0.20,        // THE PIN, in ms/frame, on the dev box, at ship dials, measured by cost-rig and
  //                   carried in BUDGET.md as well (change both or budgetsim [AB] fails). It is a
  //                   CEILING AT ~3x THE MEASUREMENT in the perf ledger's own tradition — the whole
  //                   weather measures 0.058 ms — so it catches a runaway or a quadratic turn and
  //                   never the box's weather. It is NOT a claim about anybody's laptop: the strip
  //                   prints the SHARE of the frame beside it, and a share is box-independent.
  CUT: 300 / 700,   // THE SHED RATIO. IT WAS the guard's OWN number rather than a taste call — the crash
  //                   guard's single existing action was PBUDGET 700 -> 300, this factory already deciding
  //                   how much cosmetic load a drowning frame should lose, and the sky lost the same fraction.
  //                   THAT PROVENANCE ENDED AT v1.6.679, and the number did not move with it: the guard no
  //                   longer cuts the pool (700 both ways), so 300/700 is now THE SHED'S OWN documented
  //                   fraction, inherited from the guard it used to read. The number is deliberately
  //                   unchanged — this stone raised roofs and flipped defaults, and re-deriving a fraction
  //                   nobody complained about would have been spending the director's decision for him.
  //                   `budgetsim [AB]` still holds this and `DENBUD.CUT` equal to each other; `drawsim [AS]`
  //                   rung 7 no longer divides the guard's literals, because they are now the same number.
};
// THE PASS TIMER. Every atmosphere pass is called through this, so the printed budget is the sum of
// the passes rather than a number somebody typed. Four points about it, because a meter inside the
// thing it measures deserves suspicion:
//   · IT COSTS TWO performance.now() CALLS PER PASS — seven passes, ~1 microsecond a frame against a
//     16,700 microsecond budget. Measured rather than assumed: see BUDGET.md's note.
//   · IT MEASURES THE SAME QUANTITY frameInner DOES, deliberately. Not a new meter, the same one.
//   · IN THE GATE IT READS ZERO, and that is correct rather than broken: harness/env.js hands the
//     game a FAKE clock (performance.now() returns the sim's own step counter), so two reads inside
//     one frame are identical by construction. Any law asserting a DURATION here would be measuring
//     the fixture. [AB] therefore prices the atmosphere with the sim's own hrtime, from outside.
//   · IT NEVER CHANGES WHAT IS DRAWN. atmT calls the pass with the arguments it was given and
//     returns nothing; remove the timer and the frame is identical.
//   · IT IS A BRACKET RATHER THAN A WRAPPER, and that is a deliberate second draft. The first one
//     was atmT(drawSkyClouds) — tidier to read, and it silently broke the source anchors of SIX
//     standing laws ([SS], [DT], [GS], [FL], [SD], [HS]), every one of which finds its subject by
//     looking for the literal `drawSkyClouds();` in draw(). Widening six laws written by six other
//     stones, to land one stone about milliseconds, is a bad trade and PATTERNS (6) besides. Brackets
//     on their own lines leave every one of those literals exactly where it was.
function atmIn() { ATMBUD.t0 = performance.now(); }
function atmOut() { ATMBUD.acc += performance.now() - ATMBUD.t0; }
