//
//   1 · COSMETIC POOL      `PBUDGET = 700` (:15672) + `PARTPOOL` recycle (:18745) — sparks, recycled not freed.
//   2 · THE AUTO-SCALER    `GAUGE.guard` (govGuard) — 2s under 20fps arms it; THE RED LATCH (v1.6.1419) clears it on the update half alone.
//   3 · THE ATMOSPHERE     `ATMBUD` (:15956) + `atmIn`/`atmOut`/`atmShedding`/`atmKeep` (:15994-16007).
//   4 · THE FAR TIER       `FAR` struct-of-arrays at cap 4096 (:9023), 8-way sliced tick (`farSlices` :9046).
//   5 · DISTANCE LOD       a benched pop past 2200px demotes into the far tier (the `dreamFarTick` path).
//   6 · BEHAVIOURAL LOD    `WARSCALE.curve`/`warRank()` (:1409-1424) decide how many machines run guns at a
//                          given count, and `dreamCurve()` (:1430) does the same for dives.
//
// **WHAT WAS GENUINELY MISSING, AND IT IS ONE THING: governors 4-6 are all COUNT-DRIVEN AND FRAME-BLIND.**
// `warRank(30)` returns 24 whether the frame is running at 60fps or 6. The auto-scaler knows the frame is
// drowning and its only two consumers are cosmetic: `PBUDGET`, and — since #84 — `atmShedding()`. **Nothing on
// the hostile-body side has ever read it.** `denKeep` is that wire, and it is deliberately the LAST link in an
// existing chain rather than a new chain.
//
// **IT THINS BRAINS, NEVER BODIES, AND THAT IS A RULING.** Deleting hostile bodies because the frame is slow
// would be a cheat and the director's standing order forbids it by name — *"i dont want constraints on my
// enemies and count… let the game crash if it has to."* Every body stays, every body is still lethal on contact,
// every family keeps at least one acting. What gives way is how many run a FULL brain this frame, which is
// exactly what governors 4-6 already trade and what `FAR`'s sliced tick has always done.
//
// **AND THE SELECTION STAYS NEAREST-FIRST, WHICH IS WHERE I DIFFER FROM THE ORDER, ON PURPOSE.** The order asks
// for `atmKeep`'s "spatially even" property. That property exists because a cloud is DECORATION whose position
// the pilot cannot judge — `atmKeep`'s own comment records the photograph that taught it, where keeping the
// first k kept only the clouds nobody could see. **A hostile body's position is the opposite of decoration.**
// `refreshDreamRank()` and `warRank`'s callers already sort NEAREST-FIRST, and spreading the acting bodies
// evenly across the map would idle the ones standing next to the pilot while distant ones kept their guns —
// inverting the whole point. Nearest-first is deterministic, recounted from truth on the 0.09s clock, and
// already shipped; the shed reduces the COUNT and touches nothing about the order.
//
// WHAT IT CANNOT SEE (THE HUMILITY CLAUSE): whether the frame is drowning FOR THIS REASON. It reads
// `GAUGE.guard`, which is a frame-rate fact and says nothing about the cause — a slow frame from a shader, a
// background tab or a busy box arms the same shed. And it cannot make 16 million bodies affordable: it trades
// brains at the margin, and by the director's order the tide is still allowed to sink the frame.
const DENBUD = {
  acc: 0,           // this frame's hostile-body wall, accumulated between denIn/denOut
  t0: 0,            // ...the open bracket's stamp
  ms: 0,            // ...smoothed exactly the way METER.ms and ATMBUD.ms are, and printed beside them
  SHED: 0,          // THE DIAL's wire. 1: a drowning frame thins BRAINS. 0: every body runs its full brain, always.
  //                 THE DEFAULT FLIPPED 1 -> 0 at v1.6.679 (BOOK II #82), the same ruling as ATMBUD.SHED's
  //                 and for the same reason. The wire, `denKeep`, `denShedding` and the BODY SHED dial are
  //                 all untouched; only the shipped position of the switch moved. This one mattered more
  //                 than the sky's: it was the only governor that reached the HOSTILE side, so a drowning
  //                 frame used to make the fight itself quietly easier — 30 machines acting as 11 — with
  //                 nothing on screen saying so. Now the frame drops and the fight does not. One value back.
  CAP: 3.40,        // THE PIN, ms/frame, on the dev box at ship dials, and it is DERIVED: the browser receipt
  //                   reads **0.920-1.132 ms/frame** at 562 hostile bodies (500 popcorn + 50 platinum + a 12-boy
  //                   squad - `[P26]`'s own cast) - two runs of three 160-frame windows, best-of-three each, and
  //                   the SPREAD is the point: a single run would have made 1.132 look exact. The pin is 3.00x the
  //                   HIGHER reading, because a ceiling is set from the worst honest measurement. That is the perf
  //                   ledger's own tradition: a ceiling that catches a runaway or a quadratic turn and never the
  //                   box's mood. Carried in BUDGET.md as well - change both or `budgetsim [AB]` fails. It is NOT
  //                   a claim about anybody's laptop; the strip prints the SHARE beside it and a share travels.
  // **THE BREACH LEDGER (BOOK II #158).** `SHED` is 0 by the v1.6.679 ruling above — *the frame drops and
  //   the fight does not* — and that ruling is kept, not argued with. But read its own complaint: the
  //   crime was that a drowning frame changed the fight **"with nothing on screen saying so."** SILENCE
  //   was the objection. And with the shed off, a breach now leaves **no trace at all**: `DENBUD.ms`
  //   prints live on the dev strip and is gone the next frame, so the only witness to a budget breach is
  //   somebody happening to be looking at a number during a fight.
  //   This is the cheapest possible cure and it degrades NOTHING: a count, the worst reading, and what
  //   the fight was doing at the time. **A dropped frame is a ruling; an unrecorded one is a blind spot.**
  led: { n: 0, worst: 0, phase: 0, at: -9, said: -9 },
  CUT: 300 / 700,   // THE SHED RATIO — see `ATMBUD.CUT`'s block for the whole story. In short: it WAS the crash
  //                   guard's own 700 -> 300, the guard stopped cutting at v1.6.679, and the fraction stayed put
  //                   as the shed's own documented number rather than being re-derived on the way past.
  //                   `budgetsim [AB]` still refuses to pass if this and `ATMBUD.CUT` ever disagree: #75's rule
  //                   is that a second copy of a shipped number is the copy that drifts, unless a law holds it.
};
function denIn() { DENBUD.t0 = performance.now(); }
function denOut() { DENBUD.acc += performance.now() - DENBUD.t0; }
function denShedding() { return DENBUD.SHED > 0 && GAUGE.guard; }
function denKeep(n) { return denShedding() ? Math.max(1, Math.ceil(n * DENBUD.CUT)) : n; }
function atmShedding() { return ATMBUD.SHED > 0 && GAUGE.guard; }
function atmKeep(n) { return atmShedding() ? Math.max(1, Math.ceil(n * ATMBUD.CUT)) : n; }
