//
// ---- THE RULING THIS STONE TURNS ON: "COVER" HAS TWO MEANINGS IN A SHOOTER AND THE ORDER'S LAW
// IS OWED BOTH. A prop that stops a bullet or a boot changes the fight; a prop that paints over a
// body hides the fight. Neither is answered by taste, and they are not answered the same way:
//   · THE OBSTACLE HALF IS STRUCTURAL, and it is answered by there being nothing here to collide
//     with. THERE IS NO PROP STATE IN THIS GAME — not an array, not a column, not one byte that
//     survives a frame. #80 found that a system rolling no dice cannot re-roll anybody's stream;
//     this one keeps nothing at all, so a weapon has no array to find a prop in and no state to
//     read off one. The region declares no mutable binding of its own and drawsim [SD] sweeps it.
//   · THE OCCLUSION HALF IS GEOMETRIC, and it is answered by WHERE THE PASS STANDS: in the ground
//     pass, after the sand and its weather, before the first actor in draw(). A prop pixel cannot
//     land on top of a body because every body is painted after every prop. That is #77's kind of
//     fence — structural, not measured — in the one axis this system actually has one available.
// [SD] holds both, and puts #77's own legibility instrument on top of them for the third risk the
// fences cannot reach: a prop BEHIND a body costing that body its contrast.
//
// ---- THE LAYOUT IS A PURE FUNCTION OF WHERE, AND THAT IS THE REQUIREMENT RATHER THAN A FLOURISH.
// A landmark is a thing you navigate by, so walking east past a rock and coming back must give you
// THE SAME ROCK IN THE SAME PLACE. A stream cannot promise that — its output depends on WHEN you
// drew from it, so a restage, a dial click or a re-seed hands you a different desert. A hash of the
// SLOT can, and always will. The order's "seeded palette" is therefore seeded BY THE SLOT: every
// prop's kind, size, mirror, lean and place in its cell is propHash(k, salt) and nothing else. It
// is #80's zero-dice position reached from the opposite direction — there it was the cheapest
// answer to a constitutional problem, here it is the ONLY answer to the word "landmark".
//
// ---- THE STRIDE IS THE SAND TILE'S OWN WIDTH AND NOBODY CHOSE IT FOR THIS STONE. The desert floor
// is assets/sandbox-ground.webp, 1024px wide, tiled from world x 0 by drawSandboxGround — so the
// ground already keeps a rhythm and its furniture takes one seat per beat of it. Read LIVE off the
// image rather than copied (#75: a second copy of a shipped number is the copy that drifts), and
// the pass refuses to draw at all until the tile has loaded: with no tile there is no rhythm to
// inherit, and a pinned fallback would be exactly the second copy this reads live to avoid.
// ONE LANDMARK PER TILE ON AVERAGE, NEVER ON A METRONOME. The grid is a HALF tile and a cell is
// occupied when its own number falls under DENS x CELL — so at ship exactly half the half-tiles
// carry a prop, the desert averages one per sand tile, and the gaps run irregular, which is the
// difference between a desert and a fence. It also makes the density dial slot-indexed in BOTH
// directions (#80's rung 11): turning it down removes cells and moves no survivor, turning it up
// adds cells and moves no survivor.
const PROP = {
  DENS: 1,        // THE DIAL's wire: cells occupied, as a share of the half-tile grid. 1 = one landmark
  //                 per sand tile on average; 2 fills every half-tile; 0 empties the desert.
  SZ: 1,          // THE DIAL's wire: every prop's size. THE STATURE CLAUSE is its roof — see below.
  CELL: 0.5,      // cells per sand tile. A HALF tile, so ship density is a coin flip per cell and the
  //                 spacing comes out irregular while its MEAN is still the ground's own 1024.
  INS: 0.18,      // the inset: a prop roams 0.18..0.82 of its own cell, never the seam. This is what
  //                 makes "without clutter" STRUCTURAL rather than statistical — the closest two props
  //                 can ever be is 0.36 of a cell (184.3 world px), and at BOTH dial roofs the two
  //                 widest bodies the palette can make still stand 33.3px of open sand apart. (That was
  //                 38 until #81's grade transposed the bone's box to 64 wide, making IT the widest kind
  //                 instead of the rock — drawsim [SD] rung 6 computes this number live from the tables
  //                 and prints it, so the comment can be checked rather than believed.)
  BURY: 0.08,     // the share of its own height a prop is seated UNDER the sand, so nothing in this
  //                 desert stands on a hairline. The tumbleweeds already rest their radius on the line.
};
// THE PALETTE, AND IT IS THE SAND UNDER TWO LIGHTS. Every tone in this system is SAND_COLOR moved
// along one axis in each direction and nothing else: toward CLDSHAD.TONE (#77's own fill — what an
// unlit surface in this desert is filled by, which is the SKY, which is why desert shade reads
