// ======================= THE DOWNWASH (THE-TWO-FIFTY #79) =======================
// THE ORDER: "the signature: low jetpack flight kicks a dust cone off the ground, intensity riding
// thrust; the flying man finally disturbs his world."
//
// THE ONE SENTENCE THAT MAKES THIS ITS OWN EMITTER AND NOT A FOURTH DOOR ON #78: every grain of THE
// MOVEMENT DUST is born where the PILOT is, and every grain of the downwash is born where the FLOOR is.
// #78's own fourth rung reds the day a foot raises dust 400px in the air — "there is no floor up there
// to kick" — and that clause is not in tension with this stone, it is the REASON this stone needed a
// separate door: the pilot is in the air, the dust is on the ground, and what connects them is a column
// of air. drawsim [DW]'s fourth rung is that sentence measured, and it is the rung this whole system
// would fail if anybody ever "simplified" it by calling dustTick from the airborne branch.
//
// WHAT IT INHERITS FOR FREE, which is the payoff of #78's architecture and the reason this stone is
// small: the shared pool (PARTPOOL/PBUDGET), all four bounds (the peak alpha DUST.A, the SHARE of the
// pool that tightens with PBUDGET whatever moves it, the per-frame backstop — whose #78 comment
// named THIS system by name before it existed — and the short life), both floor palettes, THE ONE
// WIND's drift, the t^2 billow in the draw, and the private stream. Nothing here is a second particle
// system. THE DUST at 0 kills the downwash too, because a stone floor is a stone floor.
//
// ---- THE RATE. This is the order's "intensity riding thrust", and it is an EXCHANGE RATE rather than a
// scaling factor, which is why nothing here is tuned. #78 inherited one number from the emitter it
// replaced: DUST.LAND = 120 px/s of arrested downward motion per grain of dust. That is the game's own
// price of dust, paid at every landing since long before this leg. A hovering jet is doing exactly what
// a landing does, except continuously and to the AIR instead of to the man: P.jetThrust.v px/s of
// downward momentum, every second, pushed into the world. So:
//
//        GRAINS PER SECOND  =  P.jetThrust.v / DUST.LAND  x  reach falloff  x  the dials
//
// At the shipped thrust that is 2900/120 = 24.2 grains a second on the deck — half again the 16/s the
// pilot's own feet lay at a full run, which is the right relation for a stone the book calls "the
// signature". HOT BURNERS' top rung (5400) pays 45/s and THE PLAYER page's roof (6000) pays 50/s, so
// "intensity riding thrust" needs no scaling clause at all: thrust IS the numerator, and every wire that
// writes thrust — the ladder, the dial, a future upgrade — writes the cone for free.
//
// AND THE BRAKE BURN IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN THAT NUMERATOR, with the arithmetic for why. The line above
// the emitter reads `player.vy -= player.vy * P.jetBrake.v * dt`, which LOOKS like more thrust and is
// not: it is a velocity-proportional damping term — drag wearing a burn's clothes — and P.jetBrake.v is
// 16, a coefficient, not an acceleration. A routine 480 px/s fall arrested by it is 7,680 px/s^2, 2.6x
// the whole jet; a dive at 1,500 px/s is 24,000, EIGHT TIMES. Folding it in would make a common input
// throw a 200-grain smoke bomb. So the wash rides the number the game itself calls thrust, and whether a
// hard arrest should kick a bigger cone is on the ledger as a question rather than smuggled in as a
// coefficient.
//
// ---- THE REACH, which is the order's word "LOW". 420 world px of altitude, and it is not my number
// either: it is the exact ceiling of SCORCHED EARTH. That clause (in the jetting branch, a few lines
// below this comment's own call site) cooks an enemy whose top is at groundY()-80 while the pilot's feet
// are within 340px above it — feet at most groundY()-420. THE GAME ALREADY DECIDED HOW FAR THIS WASH
// REACHES WHEN IT DECIDED THE WASH COULD BURN A MAN, and the sand is not entitled to a different opinion
// about the reach of the same blast than a man's scalp is. The falloff is QUADRATIC and pinned to zero at
// the reach: a free jet's centreline dynamic pressure falls with the square of the distance travelled,
// and the pinning is engineering rather than physics — a curve that did not reach zero AT the boundary
// would pop the cone off at the edge, and the pilot crosses that edge on every takeoff. w = (1 - h/R)^2
// puts half strength at 123px, which is about one body length: "low flight" in a number.
// AND THE VISIBLE PLUME IS THE COHERENCE CHECK, because dust rising off a floor no flame is touching is
// a disembodied effect. The exhaust's own grains travel sp x life = 36 to 119px down the axis from a
// throat 52px above the feet, so the longest visible tongue licks 67px below his boots. At h = 67 the
// wash is still at 0.71 strength — while you can SEE the flame reaching the floor, the floor is being
// stirred hard, and by the time the flame is nowhere near it the cone is a whisper.
//
// ---- THE CONE, in two parts, because a cone is a geometry and not a particle count.
// THE SPAWN PATCH is the blast's own intercept with the floor: the exhaust scatters +/-0.13 rad off its
// axis (the +/-0.26/2 in the emitter below), so a column leaving a throat 3px wide covers
// 3 + h*tan(0.13) px either side of the impact point — 3px on the deck, 58px at the reach. THE PATCH
// WIDENS AS HE CLIMBS WHILE THE STRENGTH FALLS, which is what a cone actually is. (dustPuff then adds
// its own per-grain scatter of r*1.1 on top, about +/-4.7px, because grains on a perfect parabola read
// as a stencil; the BOOK records the patch this function chose and the PIXELS carry both.)
// THE OUTWARD ROLL is what makes the skirt: air hits the floor and turns sideways, so a grain is thrown
// out from the impact point at DUST.WOUT x (0.35 + 0.65u), where u is how far out it was born. That
// speed is DERIVED, and its derivation lands on top of another shipped number by accident: the farthest
// grain must finish at the scorch's own half-width (75px), from a 3px throat, inside one grain's life
// (0.5s) -> (75-3)/0.5 = 144 px/s. #78's skid throws its dust backward at 150. Two derivations from
// opposite ends of the game — a damage box and a boot dragging — agree to 4%, which is the tell that both
// are describing the same sand sliding across the same floor.
// THE CENTRE IS PINNED AND THE EDGE CURLS UP, which is the shape of the thing and not a decoration. An
// impinging jet does not drag sand flat: the middle is where the column PRESSES THE FLOOR DOWN, and the
// wall jet rolling out from it curls upward at its edge. So the rise is DUST.RISE x (0.15 + 0.85u) —
// borrowed once, unscaled: THE EDGE OF THE CONE LIFTS SAND EXACTLY AS HARD AS A FOOTFALL AT A RUN, and
// the centre lifts a seventh of that. A few centre grains settle back under the line on the spawn
// jitter, which is correct rather than a defect — that is the column doing its job, and DUST.SETTLE is
// already the model of sand falling back into a floor.
// THE FIRST DRAFT USED THE SKID'S COEFFICIENT (x 0.45) on the argument that both are sand pushed
// sideways, and the photograph refused it: 7.8px of climb is a smudge, not a cone. The borrowing was
// wrong because it was not like-for-like — the skid's 0.45 is multiplied by k, which runs to 3, while the
// wash's w is roofed at 1, so "the skid's coefficient" was really the skid at a seventh of its usual
// effort. Measured, not reasoned: 21px of curtain at ship, and the arithmetic ceiling is 46 x 1.3 + 30 =
// 90 px/s, about 34px of climb, which is what #78 measured for a full dash and lives under its bar.
// THE CONE LANDS WHERE THE PLUME POINTS. The exhaust axis tips back by clamp(vx/1900, +/-0.55) rad, so
// the impact point sits h*tan(that) BEHIND the throat: hover and it is under him, fly at speed and the
// cone trails. That is the exhaust's own lean formula, read from JETW so there is no second copy of it.
const JETW = {   // THE JET'S GEOMETRY, in ONE place. #79 had to read three numbers that were literals
  //               inside the exhaust emitter; #75's lesson is that a second copy of a shipped number is
  //               the copy that drifts, so the emitter reads them from here now and the arithmetic is
  //               unchanged to the bit. These are extracted constants, not new tunables — no dial is owed.
  //               AND THE FENCE FOUND FOUR MORE COPIES THAN I KNEW ABOUT. This started as two literals
  //               lifted out of the exhaust emitter; drawsim [DW]'s source fence immediately reported the
  //               same throat spelled out in drawJetFlame, in the gunship's ember drip, in the gunship's
  //               flame column, and in the pour's sprite-local pair — five copies of the offset and three
  //               of the lean, in a system where a nozzle that moves has to move all of them at once.
  // NX WAS 28 AND THE THROAT HUNG IN OPEN AIR (v1.6.628). A cold read put a crosshair on the shipped hover
  // frame and found it *"in open sky… 4.6 to 5.6 world px away"* from the drawn pilot, level with his glove.
  // **MY OWN PROOF THAT IT WAS FINE WAS WRONG IN A WAY WORTH RECORDING:** I measured his silhouette as a
  // BOUNDING BOX (half-width 25.5) and reported the throat 1.7px inside its left edge — but at the throat’s
  // OWN ROW that box holds nothing but sky, because the box’s left edge is set by his arm eleven rows higher.
  // *A bounding box is not a body.* Re-measured the reader’s way, row by row: 2.7 world px outboard.
  // 25 puts it back on him with ~0.3px to spare, and it moves the DOWNWASH with it, which is right — the dust
  // and the flame come out of the same throat and that is the whole reason `JETW` exists.
  BELL: 3.6,       // (v1.6.1440) HALF the throat's width on the art (~7 px across under the pack's gold bell): the flame's BASE. Power lengthens the burn, it never widens the base — his note 2026-09-06: "sometimes the jet is too wide" (it grew to 30 px across under afterburn + brake burn).
  NX: 25,          // px behind the feet-origin: the yellow throat of the jetpack.
  NY: 52,          // px above the feet-origin: the same throat's height. (The gunship's idle DRIP sits at
  //                  46 on purpose — embers falling off the pilot fire, not the throat — and keeps its own.)
  LEAN: 1900,      // px/s of horizontal speed per radian the flame axis tips back.
  LEANMAX: 0.55,   // rad: how far back it may ever tip.
  SPRAY: 0.13,     // rad: HALF the flame's scatter cone, so the blast's footprint grows by tan() x height.
};
// ══ THE NOZZLE PER FRAME (v1.6.1440; his order 2026-09-05/06, verbatim: "look at all of my character sprites that use the jetpack and make
//   sure that the flame is perfectly aligned to where the jetpack thruster should go. Because in different poses, backfire, this one, that
//   one, the VFX jetpack's thrust is completely off of where it should be"). JETW.NX/NY were measured once on a hover frame and every pose
//   wore them: the backfire row's pack sits 17 px nearer the spine and 15 px higher, so the flame burned in open air beside him. The throat is
//   now READ OFF THE ART frame by frame — world px behind (the sheet's left; the mirror handles facing) and above the feet origin, the top of
//   the painted exhaust under the pack's gold bell (trax-mirror-range/jetnozzle/: the before/after sheets at x10, the crosses are these numbers).
