  // THE CLOCK SPLIT (2026-09-06, his order: "the slowing down time is effecting the gameplay, and its still slow in some places...
  //   it seems half baked, you have to finnish it"; his ruling: "the basics first... slow motion as the last reserve to slow things
  //   down just a little in some places"). THE LOOK AND THE CLOCK WERE ONE SWITCH: GAUGE.guard armed the basic paint AND the held
  //   0.60 together, so his newest wave 37 (the 1425 tap: red from second 34 to the end, 173 of 207 s) ran at x0.60 through 89 red
  //   seconds whose mean frame was UNDER 33 ms - the machine was fast and the game crawled anyway - while the wiretap sampled
  //   GAUGE.dil and read him x0.93 where the world ran x0.57. SPLIT: GAUGE.guard keeps arming the look, the dull sound and the far
  //   half-tick exactly as before (the count arm, the fps arm, his flag, his pin, the latch); the CLOCK has its own rule with NO
  //   HELD NUMBER. GOV.clk is the clamp's own dilation one-sided smoothed: it FALLS to GAUGE.dil at once (the clamp cannot be
  //   beaten - a 100 ms frame is x0.333 on the spot, a 50 ms frame x0.667, never held below the sludge) and RISES back toward 1 at
  //   GOV.RISE per wall second, so light frames under red run at x1.00 (the basics without slow motion), slow motion appears only
  //   where the frame is truly heavy, never jitters with a breathing frame, and comes home gently instead of the 0.60 -> 1.00 jump
  //   cut his 1422 run showed. govD = clk / GAUGE.dil, so the world's factor is EXACTLY GOV.clk; GOV.dil (the badge, the perf line)
  //   is GOV.clk. Off RED nothing changes: govD is 1, GOV.dil is GAUGE.dil and the referee's line receives exactly what it always
  //   did - fi-hash.js (the frame-driven world hash on the 13,823-body storm and on his wave-37 map, ESSN off because the storm's
  //   6,959 on glass would arm red by count) is IDENTICAL HEAD vs patched; under RED the world MOVES by construction - it now
  //   advances at the true clock. Laws: audiosim [CLK] THE CLOCK TABLE (off = min(1, 33.33/ms); on: 8..33 ms x1.000, 40 x0.833,
  //   50 x0.667, 100 x0.333, the rise at RISE/s with no step, the badge in both states, no stacking with the bought slow / the veil /
  //   the skybreaker hang / the death beat, GOV.FLOOR and GOV.SLUDGE_MS read by nothing) and [LATCH] clause 4; drawsim [GOVB].
  // THE PRICE OF NO HELD NUMBER (the second pass, 2026-09-06): the clock falls on EVERY heavy frame and climbs back at RISE, so one
  //   stall frame under red costs (1 - 33.3/ms) / RISE s of reduced clock, not one frame - a 100 ms hitch 1.9 s of ramp, a 130 ms one
  //   2.1 s; the 1425 tap's twenty worst frames (127-144 ms, all under red) would each start one. Re-derived PER FRAME against that tap
  //   (its rows are per-second means and worst frames, so the answer is a bracket: every frame at the second's mean clamp, one dip, or
  //   the deficit packed into frames as heavy as the worst): mean clock under red x0.573 today -> x0.67..x0.79, red seconds at or
  //   above x0.95 1 -> 59..75, 49 whole in every arrangement, game seconds lost 73.9 -> 36..57. RISE is his dial for exactly this
  //   trade: higher comes home faster and jitters more, lower is steadier and slower.
