    // character wide: draw each tile ONE px into its neighbour so the seam has nothing to fall through.
    // It cannot shift the art — the overlap is on the tile's trailing edge, which the next tile covers.
    // ══ (STONE THIRTEEN #62) THE SAME SEAM, ON THE OTHER AXIS ═══════════════════════════════════
    //   HIS ROW: *"A hard horizontal line where the sand shifts value, present in all three frames...
    //   the single most visible flaw in a still of your game."* **MEASURED, NOT EYEBALLED**
    //   (`tools/pixread.js`, written for this stone because three of its rows are claims about
    //   pixels): the step sits at ONE row, y=823 of a 900px frame, and drops 30.0 of luminance
    //   (190.7 -> 160.7) where the next sharpest edge anywhere in the frame is 9.0. Twice as hard
    //   as anything else on screen, exactly as the cold reader said.
    //   ⚠ **TWO HYPOTHESES DIED BEFORE THE RIGHT ONE, AND EACH COST ONE RENDER RATHER THAN A SHIP.**
    //     (1) the deep-sand rect at `gy + TH2` — arithmetic killed it: `groundY()` 630 + 460 is 1089,
    //         far below a 900px viewport, so that seam is not even on screen;
    //     (2) the art's own top row — cropping it changed the step by 0.00;
    //     (3) **THE BASE COAT, PROVEN BY PAINTING IT RED.** With `SAND_COLOR` swapped for `#ff0000`
    //         the row read `rgb(251,114,69)`. The base is laid at `gy` and the tile is laid at `gy`,
    //         and under a NON-INTEGER world lens the tile's leading edge resamples against it — so
    //         one row of the dark base coat prints through, full width.
    //   **THE CURE IS CURE 3'S, TURNED NINETY DEGREES.** That fix reads *"draw each tile ONE px into
    //   its neighbour so the seam has nothing to fall through"*, for the vertical hairline between
    //   tiles. This is the same defect on the horizontal edge and takes the same overlap: the tile
    //   starts one px ABOVE the ground line and carries one px more height, so the base coat has no
    //   row to show through. One px over 460 of art is below the resampler's own noise, and the
