// *"`VEIL.meter` and `VEIL.t` keep their own compensators: they are the veil's own resource and its own
// hold-timer, exact whenever the veil owns the frame, and the only case they miss is veiled-AND-hanging,
// which nothing here proves is reachable — a feel change nobody ordered is not a cure."* **Every clause of
// that was true and the conclusion was wrong, in two ways, and both were measured rather than argued:**
//   1. **VEILED-AND-HANGING IS REACHABLE IN ONE RIGHT-CLICK.** The press handler is `game.js` ~18567 —
//      `if (!shockActive() && wrunHas('shadow')) { SHADOW.holdT = 0; if (wrunHas('veil')) veilBegin(); }` —
//      and `veilBegin`'s own gate is `dead || veilActive() || shockActive() || VEIL.meter < 0.4`. **Neither
//      names `TURBO.phase`.** Fired through the shipped `mousedown` listener during a hang, `rmbVeiled` and
//      `TURBO.phase === 'hang'` stand together on the same frame, and the hang is 0.22 WORLD s = 0.63 REAL s
//      of window. Both clocks then ran at **0.77778× real** — the pilot banked 28.6% more veil than the meter
//      promised. *"Nothing proves it reachable"* was a statement about the search, not about the game.
//   2. **THE REFILL BRANCH WAS NEVER IN THE SENTENCE.** *"Exact whenever the veil owns the frame"* is a claim
//      about the DRAIN. `VEIL.meter` also moves in the `else` — the recharge — where the veil owns nothing,
//      and there it was bare `dt`: a TIME SLOW slot refilled the smoke at **0.50000× real**, no hang required.
// So both are `rdt` now, the hold-timer is one expression instead of two, and *"a feel change nobody ordered"*
// no longer applies: #116's order text asks in words for *"the veil's clocks to still read real underneath it."*
// **WHAT STILL STAYS WORLD, DECLARED SO THE NEXT STONE DOES NOT ADOPT ONE SILENTLY.** `powerT` is DECLARED WORLD by #99's fence and
// #109's census and `[TS4]` proves it still dilates. And the `gameTime`-relative windows (the 5-tap
// SKYBREAKER drumroll, the storm's jump buffer) are measured in WORLD seconds at BOTH ends, so they widen
// with the world by construction; #109's census filed them under THE REFEREE while describing them as
// scaled, which is an internal contradiction in that paragraph and is left standing rather than resolved
// by an unordered edit. Both are on `RETURN-LEDGER.md` §6.
//
// **AND `WAVEQ.clock` — THE TRICKLE SCHEDULER — IS DECLARED WORLD BY #116, WHICH IS THE FIRST TIME ANY STONE
// HAS RULED ON IT.** It appears in neither of #109's two lists, so it was riding the world's dt by the exact
// default the humility clause below warns about, unexamined. Measured: over one REAL second with a TIME SLOW
// slot armed the queue's clock advanced **0.5 s and one body arrived, against 1.0 s and two** unarmed
// (`[AB5]`). **The ruling is that this is CORRECT and it is a ruling, not an omission:** a wave's arrival
// schedule is the wave's, the reinforcements are world bodies, and a pilot who buys 0.5× buys half as many
// arrivals per real second along with everything else the world does slowly — which is the same trade
// `[ST3]`'s ×3.20 throughput roof already prices. The half a critic must state out loud is that it compounds:
// he acts twice per enemy action AND meets half the arrivals. **What it may NEVER do is ring a bell early, and
// it cannot — `wrunTick`'s whole clear block sits inside `if (WAVEQ.q.length === 0)`, asserted by `[AB5]`.**
//
// **WHAT THIS CANNOT SEE (THE HUMILITY CLAUSE).** `rdt` is a fact about the FRAME, not about correctness:
// nothing makes a new timer read it, so a referee added by a later stone still defaults to the world's
// side in silence, exactly as `pdt`'s census warns. It cannot see whether a clock SHOULD be a referee —
