// A DOOR CHANGE OR A DEATH TAKES THE SLOTS, and it is booked — the same shape `prizeClear()` uses and
// for #21's reason written in blood: a run-scoped state left armed is a bug that arrives on the NEXT
// run's first frame, and a power-up surviving a death would be exactly that.
function pactClear() { if (!PACT.slots.length) return; PACT.led.cleared += PACT.slots.length; PACT.slots.length = 0; }
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// THE HUD CHIPS — active power-ups, as timed chips (THE-TWO-FIFTY #101, LEG 5's third stone)
//
// ── IT OWNS NO STATE, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN RATHER THAN AN ECONOMY. A chip is a pure VIEW over a
// clock its own power-up already keeps: each registry row hands back `left()` (seconds remaining) and
// declares `DUR` (the full span), and this file adds no timer, no list, no tick and nothing to leak
// across a door. #102 THE STACKING RULE governs what may be ACTIVE; #101 governs what is SEEN. Those
// are different questions and a display list that could disagree with the effects it draws is the bug
// this shape makes impossible.
//
// ── PRIOR ART, and the search changed the stone. POWER SURGE is a SHIPPED timed power-up: `powerT`
// (`game.js:2411`) counts ten seconds down from `powerT = 10` at the pickup (`game.js:2432`), and it
// ALREADY HAS A READOUT — `'POWER ' + powerT.toFixed(1)`, 13px, world-space, floating above the
// pilot's head (`game.js:23697`), in `#6ef0ff` with a `#ffce54` flash under two seconds. **So this is
// not a chip system with nothing to show: `surge` is registered as row one, and the chips are live at
// the shipped default on every stage POWER SURGE reaches — which #99's stage report measured as all
// four.** The chip reuses that readout's exact two colours on purpose, so the two agree rather than
// inventing a second vocabulary for one fact.
//   · **NOTHING ABOUT `powerGem` CHANGES.** #99's criterion B fenced that pickup and the fence holds:
//     `left()` is a READ. No write, no migration onto THE BOUNCE, no behaviour touched.
//   · **THE WORLD FLOAT STAYS, and the ruling is one line.** It is not a duplicate of the chip so much
//     as the same fact at two distances — the float rides the pilot's eyes, the chip is HUD furniture
//     #102–#111 will share. Deleting a shipped readout on a stone that did not order it is the scope
//     creep the criteria fence off; whether two readouts is one too many is a FELT question and #115
//     THE FUN MEMO flies runs. Filed on the ledger rather than decided here.
//
// ── THE CORNER IS DECLARED, NOT MERELY RESERVED (criterion A). `HUDLAB.zones` gains `powerchips`
// **conditionally**, exactly the shape `wavestrip` (gated on the run) and `mirrorhud` (gated on the
// duel) already use — criterion B's own precedent. The seat is `x 8, y 98`: the third floor of the
// left column, under the cockpit (y8..52) and the shine meter (y56..94), because a pilot's own active
// states belong with his hearts and his rank and not with the war census on the right. Checked against
// every declared zone at the widths the laws actually run: `announce` opens at `W/2 − 330`, which is
// x310 at [H8]'s narrowest 1280 — so x8..84 cannot reach it. **The reservation is declared at the
// two-chip CAP even when one chip stands**, and that is deliberate: the zone's meaning is "up to MAX
// chips may stand here", which is the order's own number, and `[H22]` requires the rendered rect to sit
// INSIDE its reservation rather than to equal it.
//
// ── THE PLATE EXISTS BECAUSE THE BACKDROP IS THE WORLD. A chip must read over sand, over blue sky and
// over a muzzle flash, and `PATTERNS.md` (3)'s three cases in this tree are all a mark tuned against a
// backdrop it does not actually lie on. So each chip stands on its own dark plate at the wave strip's
// own alpha, and the ring and icon are graded against THAT, not against a guess about the sky.
//
// ── WHAT THIS CANNOT SEE (THE HUMILITY CLAUSE): it cannot see whether an effect is actually RUNNING —
// it reads a clock, and a power-up whose timer ticks while its effect has stopped would draw a
// perfectly honest chip over a lie. That is each kind's own stone to prove. It cannot see the
// TWO-CHIP case in the shipped game, because `surge` is the only registered row until #105 lands, so
// two and three chips are proven by `drawsim [PC2]` against INJECTED rows and photographed by nobody.
// And it cannot see DOM: the cockpit above it is `#hud`, laid out by the browser, and no sim here can
// measure the gap between them — only the frames can.
const PCHIP = {
  CH: 34,      // the chip box, px square
  GAP: 8,      // between chips
  X: 128,      // the third floor's RIGHT seat since v1.6.1363 — THE CORNER PAIR took the left edge on his word ('it doesnt hug the left side of the creen'); chips end x204, clear of the forge GO-UP row (x212)
  Y: 98,
  MAX: 2,      // "two chips max on screen" — the order's own number, and the reservation's width
  WARN: 2,     // seconds remaining at which the ring flashes, matching the world float's own threshold
  led: { drawn: 0, hidden: 0, live: 0 },   // the books [CH*] read; #114 audits nothing here, this file owns no economy
};
// THE REGISTRY. One row per power-up that has a CLOCK. #105/#106/#107/#109/#110/#111 each add one;
// #104's gold shield is hit-clocked rather than timed and its row is its own stone's call (a fraction
// of hits left is still a fraction, so `left`/`DUR` can carry it — said here so #104 need not re-derive
